{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n '\"'^#{1,4} .*D(439|745|747|749|750)|''^## |''^### '\"' docs/DECISIONS-log.md | tail -38; rg --files | rg 'judging-v13s.txt|PAPER-CHECK-D751|codex-r67|CURRENT_STATE|prompt-audit/SKILL'\"","aggregated_output":"10699:## D713 · 2026-11-21 · Council R passes the citation-only judge design (round 38, four passes); build judging-v12 and poc-v121 with the $0 test\n10721:## D714 · 2026-11-21 · judging-v12 and poc-v121 built; every registered $0 check passes; the paid acceptance step is Alex's\n10756:## D715 · 2026-11-22 · Alex approves $0.50 for the paid acceptance step of judging-v12\n10765:## D716 · 2026-11-22 · Council R passes the paid acceptance probe's design (round 40, six passes); the harness and its $0 rehearsal next\n10783:## D717 · 2026-11-22 · Council R passes the probe harness and its $0 rehearsal (round 41, five passes); the paid probe is sent at Alex's $0.50\n10799:## D718 · 2026-11-22 · The paid acceptance probe of judging-v12: registered verdict NOT READY; Council B recommends keeping poc-v118\n10825:## D719 · 2026-11-22 · Alex stops the citation line: judging-v12 is not adopted; poc-v118 stays the product\n10839:## D720 · 2026-11-22 · Alex opens the double-count design; the diagnosis finds the class in the frozen product too\n10859:## D721 · 2026-11-22 · Council round 43/43b: the double-count premise is partly falsified by the frozen gold; recommend closing the design with nothing to build; D711's H4 reading corrected\n10892:## D722 · 2026-11-22 · Alex closes the double-count design with nothing changed\n10905:## D723 · 2026-11-22 · Code review of 95cf8da7..3ba40d8f: two defects fixed, eight findings recorded\n10931:## D724 · 2026-11-22 · Council round 44 converges the code review: keep the v12 launcher refusal and the clean-ups, revert the link-step catch; D723 corrected\n10957:## D725 · 2026-11-22 · Council round 45/45b: the number answers fail because related numbers have no shared, checked calculation; one judge version and one test recommended to Alex\n10991:## D726 · 2026-11-22 · Alex opens a judge version for related number questions ($0 gate first) and agrees to \"Forecast unavailable\" as the first-release interim\n11008:## D727 · 2026-11-22 · judging-v13's $0 gate: the design passed council (round 46e); gate runs 1–6 and council rounds 46f–46j; the gate is not yet passed; one choice goes to Alex\n11041:## D728 · 2026-11-22 · Alex: the family judge does not see its earlier forecasts while calculating\n11055:## D729 · 2026-11-22 · judging-v13's $0 gate passes (council round 46ad); the paid gate needs its harness built at $0 and Alex's ceiling\n11073:## D730 · 2026-11-22 · Alex: build the paid gate's harness at $0, then ask for the ceiling\n11083:## D731 · 2026-11-22 · judging-v13's paid-gate harness is ready to send (council round 47h); the first attempt is scored on both arms; the ceiling is Alex's\n11110:## D732 · 2026-11-22 · Alex: the paid gate's ceiling is $0.50\n11122:## D733 · 2026-11-22 · judging-v13's paid gate: STOP, every answer refused; the format's rules were never stated in the request (council round 48); repair or close is Alex's choice\n11169:## D734 · 2026-11-22 · Alex: repair judging-v13's request and checks once, then a $0 blind conformance test\n11185:## D735 · 2026-11-22 · Council rounds 49–50: the repair is finished to a line; at most two more review rounds, then the blind test\n11210:## D736 · 2026-11-22 · Council round 51: the repair's wording review finds as many serious issues as round 49; Claude and Codex split on going back to Alex now\n11236:## D737 · 2026-11-22 · Alex: shrink judging-v13's format to the checks that stop the named defects, then a $0 blind test\n11258:## D738 · 2026-11-22 · v13s: code refuses a named defect only where the calculation's structure decides it; readers mark the five defects on every reply (council rounds 52–53)\n11290:## D739 · 2026-11-22 · v13s built and rehearsed; council round 54's findings fixed; the blind test runs, and its result goes to Council B\n11325:## D740 · 2026-11-22 · The v13s blind test stops on a checker error and a reader-marked defect; council round 55 sends three reader rulings to Alex\n11346:## D741 · 2026-11-22 · Alex rules the three reader questions of D740\n11369:## D742 · 2026-11-22 · The v13s re-run stops on one sound answer refused by S4; council 56–56c keeps S4 and makes the request state one benchmark rule; a third $0 blind run follows\n11416:## D743 · 2026-11-22 · The third v13s blind run stops on the reader brief; the writer side is clean; council 57 asks Alex to authorize a re-reading\n11449:## D744 · 2026-11-22 · The D743 re-read fixes the range rule but stops on the midpoint; one mechanism (readers lack the writer's format rules); council 58 asks Alex for one more bounded re-read under shared rule text\n11484:## D745 · 2026-11-23 · The D744 re-read calibrates the readers but stops on a qualifier-list gap and on S's unsupported share; council 59 recommends a targeted $0 test of one clarification, for Alex to authorize\n11524:## D746 · 2026-11-23 · The D745 targeted test fails on E's unsized cut; S splits on how far an indirect comparison can support a forecast; council 61–61b: one general question to Alex, then a $0 stored-answer re-read; the paid re-send stays on hold\n11573:## D747 · 2026-11-23 · Alex rules that a forecast may rest on an explained indirect comparison\n11592:## D748 · 2026-11-23 · The D747 re-read fails on a self-contradicting floor; three of four earlier splits settle under the ruling; council 63: ask Alex to authorize the E writer repair; the paid re-send stays on hold\n11641:## D749 · 2026-11-23 · Alex authorizes the E writer repair and its $0 writer test\n11656:## D750 · 2026-11-23 · The D749 writer test is INCONCLUSIVE with no failed line; council 65 (under D439): one bullet-2 sentence on reaffirmed targets, then a second $0 writer test\ndocs/CURRENT_STATE.md\ndocs/archive/2026-10-22-v15/CURRENT_STATE.md\ndocs/archive/2026-10-16-v1/CURRENT_STATE.md\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r67.stream\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r67.md\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAPER-CHECK-D751.md\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/judging-v13s.txt\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md | sed -n '440,570p'; nl -ba .claude/skills/prompt-audit/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md | sed -n '440,570p'; nl -ba .claude/skills/prompt-audit/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":"   440\t\n   441\t**[D749] The $0 writer test of the E writer repair (Alex: \"批准修写手再小测\"; council round 63), registered before any writer is\n   442\tsent.**\n   443\t- **The request change** (`judging-v13s.txt`; the D745 text is kept in `judging-v13s-d745.txt`; the registered file's sha256 is\n   444\t  `ac707fa9…`, checked by `blind5.py build` against every built system text). This is the wording after council round 64\n   445\t  (`codex-r64.md`).\n   446\t  - The last bullet's two D745 sentences are replaced by: \"Before sizing a share, deduction, discount or pace that decides the band,\n   447\t    explain which band the evidence favours. Show support for the size or a justifying bound. If the passages lack the quantity asked,\n   448\t    explain why a related figure fits the companies, period and quantity, and why your assumptions favour that band. If you can say\n   449\t    only that the outcome might fall short or overshoot, not which band is more likely, answer unresolved and name what is unknown.\"\n   450\t  - Bullet 3's \"is an input to be discounted for time and execution (…), not an answer\" becomes \"is an input, not an answer: weigh\n   451\t    time and execution (…) under the evidence requirement below\". Council 64 found that the old imperative invited the old order\n   452\t    (discount first) on E.\n   453\t  - Sources:\n   454\t    - D745: show the support (council 64: \"give its size\" was narrower than D745, so \"show support for the size\");\n   455\t    - D747: an explained related figure counts, only when the passages lack the quantity asked (council 64: the first wording was\n   456\t      wider);\n   457\t    - D749: decide the band before sizing, else unresolved, naming the unknown;\n   458\t    - D745's ruling: a justified unresolved answer is usable.\n   459\t  - Size: 4,540 → 4,794 characters, 910 → 961 tokens (o200k_base). The writer's request now carries the same routes the readers judge\n   460\t    by, and the decision order Alex approved.\n   461\t  - Checks at $0: cases 111 of 111, rehearsal 19 of 19, and the pinned v13 plan unchanged (`paid-plan.sha256`). The schema and the code\n   462\t    are unchanged.\n   463\t- **Scope** (`blind5.py`; run folder `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/`):\n   464\t  - cases S, E and P-v8, built from the repaired request;\n   465\t  - one fresh Claude subagent and one fresh read-only Codex session per case, 6 writers, each reading only `system.txt`, `user.txt` and\n   466\t    `schema.json`.\n   467\t- **Readers.** 8 blinded packets, shuffled X01–X08, with the key in `reader-key.json`:\n   468\t  - the 6 fresh replies;\n   469\t  - 2 controls carried unchanged from the targeted test, each shown with the request it was written for;\n   470\t  - each packet read by one fresh Claude and one fresh Codex reader under the D747 definitions.\n   471\t\n   472\t  Where they disagree, on support or on any defect, two adjudicators read the packet, one per family. The reasons behind every\n   473\t  settled vote are recorded by Claude as compatible or not (`reasons.json`). Matching votes with incompatible reasons go to\n   474\t  adjudication.\n   475\t\n   476\t  | Control | Built from | Expected reading |\n   477\t  |---|---|---|\n   478\t  | K-invented-cut | blind4 U05, E Codex: 90% of $40B with no order size or shipment pace | not supported |\n   479\t  | K-impossible-floor | blind4 U02, S Claude: \"more than $730B\" called a floor above its own $720B total | \"part above or added into its whole\" on F12, settled; its support is not scored |\n   480\t- **Pass:**\n   481\t  - every fresh S and E reply has a settled yes, on its forecast or on its unresolved reason;\n   482\t  - both fresh P-v8 replies have a settled yes on a forecast;\n   483\t  - every fresh reply is accepted and publishable by the code;\n   484\t  - no settled defect on a fresh reply;\n   485\t  - both controls read as expected.\n   486\t- **Otherwise (tally rules after council 64):**\n   487\t  - **Fail:**\n   488\t    - a fresh reply refused or held by the code (decided before any reading; an unreadable reply builds no packet);\n   489\t    - a fresh P-v8 reply that is not a forecast (decided from its shape alone);\n   490\t    - a settled no on a fresh reply;\n   491\t    - a settled defect on a fresh reply;\n   492\t    - K-invented-cut not settled no;\n   493\t    - the floor defect marked by neither reader, or rejected by both adjudicators.\n   494\t  - **Inconclusive:** a support split, a defect split on a fresh reply, or a floor split left after adjudication.\n   495\t  - **The floor control** is matched on its type and on naming F12, at both stages, so readers who list the other figures differently\n   496\t    still agree. Adjudicator votes are validated like first votes.\n   497\t  - Either outcome goes back to the council, and the paid re-send stays on hold.\n   498\t  - Only a clean pass supports asking Alex to authorize the paid re-send.\n   499\t- **Checked on a scratch copy before the send** (the targeted test's six replies stood in as fresh replies):\n   500\t  - a clean set passes, with the floor marked by readers who listed its figures differently;\n   501\t  - a fresh defect marked by one reader waits for adjudication;\n   502\t  - a fresh defect still split after adjudication is inconclusive;\n   503\t  - the invented-cut control read as supported fails;\n   504\t  - the floor missed by both readers fails;\n   505\t  - a fresh P-v8 answered unresolved fails;\n   506\t  - a fresh reply that is not JSON fails before any reading.\n   507\t- **Known limits, disclosed now:**\n   508\t  - Readers split on boundary answers (D748). A fresh answer on a boundary can leave the run inconclusive even when the repair works.\n   509\t  - All four fresh E and S replies may pass as justified \"unresolved\". A pass therefore shows the repair stops invented sizes. It\n   510\t    does not show how often the writer withholds on boundary cases (council 64).\n   511\t\n   512\t**[D750] The second $0 writer test (council round 65; under D439 while Alex is away), registered before any writer is sent.**\n   513\t- **Why.** The D749 test was INCONCLUSIVE, with no failed line (`BLIND5-RESULT.md`).\n   514\t  - X05, E Claude, took a not-yet-shipping target in full, asserting \"already selling at volume\". None of E's 15 passages shows\n   515\t    shipments, revenue to date or orders.\n   516\t  - X08, S Claude: its readers disagreed on whether \"more than $730B\" of AI infrastructure is a floor above its own $710.4B outcome.\n   517\t- **The request change** (`judging-v13s.txt`; the D749 text is kept in `judging-v13s-d749.txt`; the registered sha256 `54f5847d…` is\n   518\t  checked by `blind6.py build` against every built system text). Bullet 2's \"it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the\n   519\t  volume in hand\" becomes \"it remains a forecast when reaffirmed, and is taken in full only when you show what evidence puts the\n   520\t  required volume in hand for the period\".\n   521\t  - This is council 65's wording. Claude's closed list was rejected: an order with a date can cover one unit, and a list would\n   522\t    exclude the request's own disclosed-comparable route and D747's explained comparison.\n   523\t  - Size: 4,794 → 4,867 characters, 961 → 975 tokens.\n   524\t  - Checks: cases 111 of 111, rehearsal 19 of 19, and the pinned v13 plan unchanged.\n   525\t- **Design.** D749's, unchanged (`blind6.py` wraps `blind5.py`), on a new run folder, `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23/`:\n   526\t  - S, E and P-v8, each with one fresh Claude and one fresh read-only Codex writer;\n   527\t  - the same two reader controls (K-invented-cut, K-impossible-floor);\n   528\t  - fresh readers of both families, with adjudication where they disagree;\n   529\t  - the same pass, fail and inconclusive lines.\n   530\t- **One added reading rule** for the reasons record (council 65), applied by Claude before any vote counts as settled. On S, a reader\n   531\t  citing a related figure as a floor above the answer's own outcome is not compatible with a reader treating it as a guide. Such a\n   532\t  pair goes to adjudication.\n   533\t- **What the result decides:**\n   534\t  - a clean pass supports asking Alex to authorize the paid re-send;\n   535\t  - a settled unsupported answer or defect fails;\n   536\t  - a persistent split stays inconclusive and goes back to the council.\n   537\t- **Changes from council round 66** (`codex-r66.md`), made before any writer was sent. The writer sentence is kept. The tally in\n   538\t  `blind5.py`, shared by `blind6.py`, gains three changes:\n   539\t  - **The decisive premise.** For a fresh S or E reply, a settled yes needs the reasons record to name the decisive premise and what\n   540\t    in the passages supports it, in a `premise` field (`adjudicated_premise` after adjudication).\n   541\t    - On E: what puts the volume in hand for the period, when the writer takes the target in full.\n   542\t    - On S: how the writer's use of a related figure fits its own dollar outcome.\n   543\t    - Two readers agreeing on an unsupported premise, such as both reading a floor above the outcome, go to adjudication. If both\n   544\t      final approvals still rest on it, the support stays unsettled.\n   545\t  - **K-invented-cut** fails on a split too, as registered: it must earn a settled no.\n   546\t  - **A defect an adjudicator leaves undecided** is pending, not a split.\n   547\t  - Checked on a scratch copy: no recorded premise gives no verdict; with premises it passes; a split invented-cut control fails; an\n   548\t    omitted floor decision waits.\n   549\t  - D749's `TALLY.json` stands as scored under its own registration. Re-running it under the new rules would ask for premise records\n   550\t    it was not registered to need.\n     1\t---\n     2\tname: prompt-audit\n     3\tdescription: After every measurement of an extraction configuration, and before any prompt change, map each failure class in the stored draws to the sentence of the request that produced it, check that sentence against the frozen standard and the prompt principles, and write the audit table. Alex, 2026-11-02 - \"你应该看着prompt，然后对比失败的trace，然后仔细的根据尺子做对比，并且我们prompt的principle也要带入，是尝试教他general hedge fund analyst skill，不是要hardcode或是benchmark farming；你把这个写成一个skill以后固定跑\".\n     4\t---\n     5\t\n     6\t# Prompt audit: failures → the request's sentences → the standard → the principle\n     7\t\n     8\tRun this after every measured configuration (three draws scored on the frozen ruler) and before any edit to a prompt file, a schema\n     9\tdescription or a takes line. A measurement without this audit is a score without a diagnosis (AGENTS §7.2, §6). The audit is read,\n    10\tnot scripted: the scripts below only produce the lists to read.\n    11\t\n    12\t## 0. Inputs\n    13\t\n    14\t- The arm's run folders (`docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/arms/<arm>/d1..3`) and the baseline's.\n    15\t- The frozen ruler (AGENTS §7.1): the extraction standard `docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html` (version 3, D243, with D250's\n    16\t  boundaries), golden-v5, the questions standard, the judgement gold. Never edited; every disagreement is a gap in the system.\n    17\t- The prompt principles (AGENTS §6 and §8): the prompt carries the standard and no new rule; it teaches a general hedge-fund\n    18\t  analyst, so every sentence and example reads the same for a bank, a drug maker, a retailer or a chip maker, names no covered\n    19\t  company, product or number, and takes no example from a test article; a classification is a closed set with examples; never grow a\n    20\t  prompt to fix quality without cutting as much elsewhere; a field's definition sits in the schema beside the field; code owns\n    21\t  identity, never importance.\n    22\t\n    23\t## 1. Dump the request as the model reads it\n    24\t\n    25\tOne stored request of the configuration (any article), printed whole: the system text (the role), every user block before the item\n    26\t(category block, subjects, research reference, open questions with their takes lines, research groups), the closing sentence, the\n    27\tend-of-request check, and every schema field description (evidence and candidates). Read it top to bottom once. List every\n    28\tinstruction that decides keep, reject, link, open, or a field value, with its location (role paragraph, check row, schema field,\n    29\ttakes line, category block). This list is the thing the failures are mapped onto.\n    30\t\n    31\t```sh\n    32\tpython3 - <<'PY'\n    33\timport sqlite3, json\n    34\ta = sqlite3.connect('file:<run folder>/archive.sqlite?mode=ro', uri=True)\n    35\treq = json.loads(a.execute(\"select request_json from model_calls where purpose='extract' and request_json like '%<title fragment>%' order by call_id desc limit 1\").fetchone()[0])\n    36\tprint(req['messages'][0]['content']); print(req['messages'][1]['content'].split('[Item]')[0]); print(req['messages'][1]['content'].rsplit('\\n\\n', 2)[-2:])\n    37\tprint(json.dumps({k: v.get('description') for k, v in req['response_format']['json_schema']['schema']['properties']['evidence']['items']['properties'].items() if v.get('description')}, ensure_ascii=False, indent=1))\n    38\tPY\n    39\t```\n    40\t\n    41\t## 2. Produce the failure lists (all at no model cost, on the stored draws)\n    42\t\n    43\t| List | Script | What it gives |\n    44\t|---|---|---|\n    45\t| Matter states across draws | `harness/matter_states.py arm=d1,d2,d3 base=d1,d2,d3` | every gold core/supporting matter: extracted or reaching in 3/2/1/0 draws; the systematic misses (0 of 3) and the coin flips (1 of 3) |\n    46\t| Written noise by the standard's row | the `rows_all` count in `design-v72.md` §10 (the gold's `not_kept[].row`) | which row of the standard the written noise falls under, with examples |\n    47\t| No-home drops | `harness/dropped_census.py d1 d2 d3` | gold matters written but dropped, with the questions open at the time |\n    48\t| Where links go | `harness/links_by_question.py d1 d2 d3 --sample 20` | the pile per question, the revenue-sink share, statements under several questions, noise under revenue |\n    49\t| Quote refusals | the `dropped` reasons of the handovers (`quote not in item`), each diffed against its closest source window | paraphrase against a word or two off |\n    50\t| Question recall | `harness/question_recall.py d1 d2 d3` | must_open / may_open covered by seeds and by article-opened questions |\n    51\t| Fields | 15 random stored statements with speaker, original_who, original_day, tier | read against the quote and the item's day and publisher |\n    52\t\n    53\tFor every list, read the items, not the counts: the raw answer beside the article for every systematic miss (a miss the scorer reports\n    54\tmay be the matter written in other words: check before counting it), a sample of twenty for every pile.\n    55\t\n    56\t## 3. Map each failure class to the request\n    57\t\n    58\tFor each class with more than a handful of cases, fill one row:\n    59\t\n    60\t| Failure class (count, share) | The sentence(s) of the request that produced it, quoted | The standard's line on the same case (section, decision) | Verdict | The model's own account (when the sentence is not obvious) |\n    61\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n    62\t\n    63\tVerdicts, one of: **contradiction** (two sentences of the request pull opposite ways, or a request sentence removes what the standard\n    64\tkeeps, D240), **missing** (no sentence decides the case; the model guesses, a coin flip per send), **abstract** (a clause without an\n    65\texample, read differently each send), **misread** (the sentence is right, the model does not apply it; needs the model's own account\n    66\tbefore any change), **not the prompt** (code, scorer or sampler).\n    67\t\n    68\tThe model's own account: continue the stored conversation with one question about one case (`harness/probe_v72_link.py`,\n    69\t`probe_v72_missing.py`), a cent a case, for every \"misread\" and for any class whose sentence is not obvious from reading.\n    70\t\n    71\t## 4. Check every proposed fix against the principles before writing it\n    72\t\n    73\t- It is written from the standard's line, not from the failing article: the example comes from another domain, names no covered\n    74\t  company, product or number, and reads the same for every industry. A fix that only recovers the bench's misses is benchmark\n    75\t  farming and is not written.\n    76\t- It removes a contradiction, supplies a missing definition at its owner (schema field, takes line, check row, category block), or\n    77\t  replaces an abstract clause with a closed set and examples. It never adds a rule the standard does not hold.\n    78\t- It cuts at least as much as it adds, or the growth has a written reason; report characters before and after.\n    79\t- One class per change; the change is proved on the stored requests of that class, three sends as stored against three changed,\n    80\t  with the noise guards of the same articles; the whole bench accepts it (three draws) only when Alex asks for a run.\n    81\t\n    82\t## 5. Output\n    83\t\n    84\tWrite the table and the verdicts into the arm's design document (a numbered section), one row per class, in the order of the loss;\n    85\tthe report to Alex states each class in a reader's words (what the system does wrong, which sentence makes it do that, what the\n    86\tstandard says), never in the loop's own words (AGENTS, \"Authority and scope\": explain as to a sixteen-year-old).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/judging-v13s.txt; nl -ba docs/DECISIONS-log.md | sed -n '11484,11524p;11558,11596p;11638,11715p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/judging-v13s.txt; nl -ba docs/DECISIONS-log.md | sed -n '11484,11524p;11558,11596p;11638,11715p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\tYou are a senior hedge-fund analyst judging the current rungs of a family of related number theses: one company's quantities that overlap, such as a total and its segment, a level and its growth, or a later year that rests on an earlier year's base. Use only the supplied evidence and themes in force. Each rung is your forecast: the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved.\n     2\t\n     3\t- Judge every member from the evidence shown (what recent judgements relied on and what arrived recently) and the themes in force, as if for the first time. Earlier forecasts are not shown: whether a conclusion changed is compared afterwards.\n     4\t- Judge each member against a reference: what the market expects for that question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on its ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate whose pace you forecast, informed by guidance, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted subject to the evidence requirement below; it remains a forecast when reaffirmed, and is taken in full only when you show what evidence puts the required volume in hand for the period. Build the family's figures once and read every member's outcome from them: a part cannot exceed its whole, a figure that spans several periods or products gives a question only its share, and a quantity the family shares has one forecast. Compare each outcome with its rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\n     5\t- Weigh by tier and by novelty. Only fact can establish that a rung's outcome already holds; \"not established\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. Guidance for the current or next quarter is the strongest forecast input. A management target for a product not yet shipping, or an ambition spanning years, is an input, not an answer: weigh time and execution (how many selling quarters remain, what volume and price the figure implies, what the company's own choices say) under the evidence requirement below; consensus is where the market stands, not the answer either. third_party_view and rumour move the forecast in proportion to their specificity (a number, a date, a named party): a research firm's shipment or revenue tracking with a number counts. A commitment or deal that is announced but not sized is sized from its disclosed comparable (the same programme's other deal, scaled by capacity or units); a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it. A repeat, another outlet for the same claim, or an opinion that adds no new fact or calculation moves nothing. Missing confirmation alone is not grounds for the lowest or highest rung: an event not being confirmed is not confirmation that it did not happen.\n     6\t- An evidence item that replaces another includes its predecessor for context, such as guidance revised from 12% to 25% growth. The predecessor is no longer evidence in force.\n     7\t- \"Themes in force\" lists the library's open theme theses (policy stages, shared quantities) with their current conclusions. They are our own judgements, never evidence: do not cite them in cites. Weigh each one that bears on a member and report it in that member's theme_pushes with the direction it pushes that member (toward_first, toward_last or none); leave out a theme that does not bear on it. A theme marked \"updated since this thesis was last judged\" may change the conclusion; one that is not marked already stood at the previous judgement. When no themes are listed, theme_pushes is empty.\n     8\t- Your conclusions must be consistent with each other and with the themes in force as shown: a share cannot be judged at zero while the policy that gates it is judged likely to open.\n     9\t- Return the family's figures and one answer per member, as the JSON schema defines them. Every number an answer rests on is a figure; a number you judge rather than read or compute is an estimate, stated as yours. Before sizing a share, deduction, discount or pace that decides the band, explain which band the evidence favours. Show support for the size or a justifying bound. If the passages lack the quantity asked, explain why a related figure fits the companies, period and quantity, and why your assumptions favour that band. If you can say only that the outcome might fall short or overshoot, not which band is more likely, answer unresolved and name what is unknown. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\n 11484\t## D745 · 2026-11-23 · The D744 re-read calibrates the readers but stops on a qualifier-list gap and on S's unsupported share; council 59 recommends a targeted $0 test of one clarification, for Alex to authorize\n 11485\t\n 11486\t- **The result** (`docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND3-RESULT.md` §5; `REREAD2-TALLY.json`):\n 11487\t  - **The readers are calibrated.**\n 11488\t    - All five controls passed: midpoint, 108.5 misread, overlap, disjoint, unsupported assumption.\n 11489\t    - No defect was marked by both readers on the 12 replies.\n 11490\t  - **STOP on two lines, as written:**\n 11491\t    1. T10 (S, Claude writer) F11: \"an estimated $750 billion\" recorded with qualifier \"none\". Both adjudicators upheld it. \"estimated\" is\n 11492\t       not in the closed qualifier list, so the format forced \"none\". This is 1 affected figure of 251 source figures in the three writer\n 11493\t       runs.\n 11494\t    2. S has zero usable replies. Both writers state a data-center share of total capex (90%; 95–100%) that the passages do not size, and\n 11495\t       the share decides the band.\n 11496\t  - **E's Codex reply** is now \"not supported\": its 15% cut from the guided floor decides the band. This is the same weakness.\n 11497\t- **Council round 59** (`codex-r59.md`) chose a targeted $0 test, (c), over Claude's lean to go to Alex for the paid re-send, (b):\n 11498\t  - Going straight to the paid re-send would measure the known weakness in gpt-6-luna without the request saying what a supported size\n 11499\t    is.\n 11500\t  - Skipping a clean result needs Alex to change the registered sequence anyway.\n 11501\t  - Claude agrees: the weakness is unsupported precision in a number that decides the rung, which is the goal's own failure (D725).\n 11502\t- **Proposed test (for Alex to authorize):**\n 11503\t  1. Add \"estimated\" to the qualifier list, and show readers the allowed values. Prove it on the saved replies, with a case and a\n 11504\t     mutant.\n 11505\t  2. Add one clarification to the existing instruction: when a deduction or cut decides the band, show the evidence for its size or a\n 11506\t     bound the passages support that justifies the band; if neither, answer unresolved.\n 11507\t  3. Test on S and on one stored case where the passages do support a narrower figure.\n 11508\t  4. Before the test, Alex rules whether a justified unresolved answer counts as usable.\n 11509\t  - Falsifier: a fresh S answer still picks a band on an unsupported share, or the supported case is withheld.\n 11510\t- **Alex's answers** (2026-11-23):\n 11511\t  - to \"怎么走？\": \"批准针对性小测 (推荐)\". The other options were \"不小测，直接议花钱重发\" and \"先停在这里\".\n 11512\t  - to whether a justified \"无法判断\" counts: \"算合格 (推荐)\". The other option was \"不算\".\n 11513\t- **Decided (Alex):**\n 11514\t  1. Run the targeted $0 test.\n 11515\t  2. An unresolved answer whose reason shows the passages cannot settle the rung counts as a usable answer.\n 11516\t- **Council round 60** (`codex-r60.md`) set the wording and the registration (`SMALL-DESIGN.md` §5 [D745]):\n 11517\t  - Rung-deciding shares, deductions and discounts must show their passage support or a justifying bound, else unresolved.\n 11518\t  - The default discount is made subject to that rule.\n 11519\t  - The three controls are the saved E 15% cut, a vague P-v8 unresolved answer and a justified S unresolved answer (expected no, no,\n 11520\t    yes).\n 11521\t  - Pass: every S and E reply settled yes on a forecast or its reason; both P-v8 replies settled yes on a forecast. A support split\n 11522\t    is inconclusive.\n 11523\t\n 11524\t## D746 · 2026-11-23 · The D745 targeted test fails on E's unsized cut; S splits on how far an indirect comparison can support a forecast; council 61–61b: one general question to Alex, then a $0 stored-answer re-read; the paid re-send stays on hold\n 11558\t    comparison applies to the companies, the period and the quantity asked, and why, under its own assumptions, that side of the line\n 11559\t    is more likely.\n 11560\t  - **The one test after his ruling.** A $0 blinded re-read of nine stored answers under his words, added to the shared definitions:\n 11561\t    - the answers: U01–U06, U08, the third run's T10 (for its spending argument only), and T15 (the accepted E bracket);\n 11562\t    - fresh readers, one per family;\n 11563\t    - what his choice implies for each answer is recorded before reading.\n 11564\t    - It moves on to a writer repair only if both families apply his words alike. A continuing split leaves the publication question\n 11565\t      open in practice. A clean re-read does not turn D745 into a pass.\n 11566\t  - **The paid re-send stays on hold** until an E writer repair passes a clean $0 test. The proposed repair: the writer chooses between\n 11567\t    a forecast and \"can't tell\" before it assigns a discount, naming what makes one side more likely under its own assumptions. It is a\n 11568\t    new arm and goes to council before any send. Publishing management's figure whenever it sits on a cutoff would change the product,\n 11569\t    and needs Alex's own decision.\n 11570\t- **The question to Alex** (pending): \"资料没直接给出所问的数。用一个相关数字作比较，能解释为什么结果更可能在线的这一边，但另一边仍有可能。这时可以发布预测，还是要等到能从资料算出结果落在线的哪一边？\"\n 11571\t- **Not done.** Nothing was paid. Nothing was tuned on the 36 stored answers. The request was not changed after the registration.\n 11572\t\n 11573\t## D747 · 2026-11-23 · Alex rules that a forecast may rest on an explained indirect comparison\n 11574\t\n 11575\t- **The question** (after D746): \"资料没直接给出题目问的那个数，只能拿一个相关的数字来比。比如问四大云厂商今年的数据中心开支能不能过 7000 亿，资料只说“这四家 AI 基础设施开支超过 7300 亿”。这种比较能说明结果更可能落在线的某一边，但另一边也还有可能。这时系统可以发布预测吗，还是要等资料能直接算出落在哪边？\"\n 11576\t  - Options:\n 11577\t    - \"可以发布，要讲清楚 (推荐)\": the answer must explain why the comparison applies to the companies, the year and the quantity\n 11578\t      asked, and why, under its own assumptions, that side is more likely; an answer that cannot explain it is not supported;\n 11579\t    - \"先说“还判断不了”\";\n 11580\t    - \"先停在这里\".\n 11581\t- **Alex's answer** (2026-11-23): \"可以发布，要讲清楚 (推荐)\".\n 11582\t- **Decided (Alex).** When the passages do not give the asked quantity, a forecast may rest on a related figure. It is supported\n 11583\t  only when the answer explains two things: why the comparison applies to the companies, the period and the quantity asked; and why,\n 11584\t  under the answer's own stated assumptions, the chosen side of the line is more likely. An answer that cannot explain this is not\n 11585\t  supported.\n 11586\t  - D741 ruling 3 still holds: a total used for a narrower quantity states the deduction as its own figure, or gives a supported\n 11587\t    bound.\n 11588\t  - D745's ruling still holds: a justified unresolved answer counts as usable.\n 11589\t- **Next (D746).** The ruling's words go into the shared reader definitions (`blind_rules.py`). Then one $0 blinded re-read of nine\n 11590\t  stored answers, registered with its expected readings and reviewed by the council before any packet is read.\n 11591\t\n 11592\t## D748 · 2026-11-23 · The D747 re-read fails on a self-contradicting floor; three of four earlier splits settle under the ruling; council 63: ask Alex to authorize the E writer repair; the paid re-send stays on hold\n 11593\t\n 11594\t- **The result** (`docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND4-RESULT.md` §6; `REREAD-TALLY.json`, `reread-reasons.json`):\n 11595\t  - **FAIL on the stop line.** On U02 (S, Claude writer), both adjudicators upheld \"part above or added into its whole\". The answer\n 11596\t    calls \"more than $730B\" of AI infrastructure \"the supported floor\" for data-center spending, while its own total spending is $720B\n 11638\t      asking Alex for the paid re-send.\n 11639\t- **Not done.** Nothing was paid. No request text was changed.\n 11640\t\n 11641\t## D749 · 2026-11-23 · Alex authorizes the E writer repair and its $0 writer test\n 11642\t\n 11643\t- **The question** (after D748): \"下一步怎么走？\"\n 11644\t  - Options:\n 11645\t    - \"批准修写手再小测 (推荐)\": the writer first decides whether the evidence favours one side of the line, then whether to discount;\n 11646\t      if it can only say sales might fall short, it answers \"can't tell\" and says what is missing. The changed request goes to the\n 11647\t      council, then one $0 writer test on E, S and P with the two failed answers as controls. A clean pass comes back to Alex for the\n 11648\t      paid re-send.\n 11649\t    - \"不修，直接议花钱重发\".\n 11650\t    - \"先停在这里\".\n 11651\t- **Alex's answer** (2026-11-23): \"批准修写手再小测 (推荐)\".\n 11652\t- **Decided (Alex).** Repair the writer request as council 63 described. Review it in council before any send. Then run one $0\n 11653\t  blind writer test on E, S and P-v8, with the saved U05 (invented cut) and U02 (impossible floor) as reader controls, under D748's\n 11654\t  pass lines. The paid re-send stays on hold until that test passes clean.\n 11655\t\n 11656\t## D750 · 2026-11-23 · The D749 writer test is INCONCLUSIVE with no failed line; council 65 (under D439): one bullet-2 sentence on reaffirmed targets, then a second $0 writer test\n 11657\t\n 11658\t- **Alex's direction** (2026-11-23, before sleeping): \"你接下来开始自行收敛，方向吧，我要准备睡了，所以这边的决策交给你，还有 Council， 记得不要 Scope Drift， 我们的目标是把剩下有问题的地方收敛起来，然后准备要往 Production 方向去走了，所以你自己每次复习的时候看 Log， 看我们试过了哪些东西，不要犯一模一样的错误，然后赶紧的往收敛方向去做测量和把它定下来。\"\n 11659\t  - D439 governs: the council decides reversibly, no paid send (its ceiling is spent), nothing committed.\n 11660\t- **The result** (`docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND5-RESULT.md`; run `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/`):\n 11661\t  - **The repair worked where it was aimed.**\n 11662\t    - E's Codex writer, which invented a cut in both earlier runs, now answers a justified unresolved, accepted by both families.\n 11663\t    - Both P-v8 replies are supported forecasts.\n 11664\t    - Both controls were caught: the invented cut settled no, and the impossible floor was upheld by both adjudicators.\n 11665\t    - No fresh defect, no refusal. S's Codex forecast is supported.\n 11666\t  - **Two splits:**\n 11667\t    - X05, E Claude, takes a not-yet-shipping target in full, asserting \"already selling at volume\". No passage shows shipments,\n 11668\t      revenue to date or orders.\n 11669\t    - X08, S Claude: one reader cites \"more than $730B\" as a floor above its own $710.4B outcome, the other reads it as a guide.\n 11670\t- **Claude's error, corrected.** I first recorded X08's reasons as compatible although my own note named the tension. Council 65\n 11671\t  caught it. X08 was then adjudicated as registered and stayed split.\n 11672\t- **Council round 65** (`claude-r65.md`, `codex-r65.md`) reached consensus:\n 11673\t  - X05's premise is a question of fact under the existing rule, not a gap in Alex's words.\n 11674\t  - Claude's closed list of \"volume in hand\" was rejected: it was wider and narrower than the standard at once.\n 11675\t  - The smaller sentence is at bullet 2: \"it remains a forecast when reaffirmed, and is taken in full only when you show what evidence\n 11676\t    puts the required volume in hand for the period\".\n 11677\t  - One $0 writer test on all three cases (Alex's three-case condition), with the same controls and lines. On S, a reading that cites a\n 11678\t    floor above the answer's own outcome is not compatible with one that reads a guide.\n 11679\t  - The paid re-send stays on hold.\n 11680\t- **Done.** The sentence was changed (4,794 → 4,867 characters). Cases 111 of 111, rehearsal 19 of 19, pinned plan unchanged. The test\n 11681\t  is registered (`SMALL-DESIGN.md` §5 [D750]) and built by `blind6.py`. Council round 66 reviews it before any send.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'for d in poc/data/judging-v13s-blind{3,4,5,6}-*/E; do wc -lc \"$d\"/{user.txt,answers/*.json}; shasum \"$d/user.txt\"; done; nl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-*/E/user.txt; tail -n 3 docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/log.md; sed -n '\"'158,300p' docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'for d in poc/data/judging-v13s-blind{3,4,5,6}-*/E; do wc -lc \"$d\"/{user.txt,answers/*.json}; shasum \"$d/user.txt\"; done; nl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-*/E/user.txt; tail -n 3 docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/log.md; sed -n '\"'158,300p' docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md\"","aggregated_output":"      59   13109 poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/user.txt\n     188    8447 poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/answers/claude.json\n     203    6544 poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/answers/codex.json\n     450   28100 total\n5c123d4f2ade8bb7f36123109f4d03111dfffd2c  poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/user.txt\n      59   13109 poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/user.txt\n     188    9740 poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json\n     170    4951 poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json\n     417   27800 total\n5c123d4f2ade8bb7f36123109f4d03111dfffd2c  poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/user.txt\n      59   13109 poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/E/user.txt\n       1    6687 poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json\n      92    4067 poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json\n     152   23863 total\n5c123d4f2ade8bb7f36123109f4d03111dfffd2c  poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/E/user.txt\n      59   13109 poc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23/E/user.txt\n       1    7998 poc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json\n     107    4052 poc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json\n     167   25159 total\n5c123d4f2ade8bb7f36123109f4d03111dfffd2c  poc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23/E/user.txt\n     1\t[Family] members:\n     2\tThesis v41 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA Vera CPU revenue\", FY2028, due 2028-02-29): What will NVIDIA report as Vera CPU revenue for FY2028?\n     3\tLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $50 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $40 billion and less than $50 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $30 billion and less than $40 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $20 billion and less than $30 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is less than $20 billion.\n     4\t\n     5\tRelations: none.\n     6\tShared quantities, one forecast figure each; on every arithmetic path from an outcome, through rates and multiples too, the first figure of that quantity reached is its forecast: none.\n     7\tOutcome keys (population | measure | scope | period), in USD bn, to be used exactly: v41 = NVIDIA | revenue | Vera CPU | FY2028.\n     8\t\n     9\t[Evidence in view for the family, 15 items]\n    10\t- e3660 [party_statement; Bram Berkowitz, fool.com, reporting Colette Kress's statement; original source NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress ; from gnews:fd25a8ea6f60290264bfbe55 2026-08-30] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said NVIDIA still expects $20 billion of CPU revenue in FY2027 and expects FY2028 CPU revenue to more than double that amount.\n    11\t    Quote: The bad news for AMD and Intel is that Kress said Nvidia not only still expects $20 billion in CPU revenue in its fiscal 2027, but expects that number to more than double in fiscal 2028.\n    12\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3302, e4691, e4856; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    13\t- e4687 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source AMD ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] AMD estimates that server CPU market revenue could grow at a 50% annual rate through 2030, reaching a $220 billion opportunity by the end of the decade.\n    14\t    Quote: AMD notes that the server CPU market's revenue could increase at an annual rate of 50% through 2030, creating a $220 billion revenue opportunity by the end of the decade.\n    15\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4084; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    16\t- e4689 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst), quoting Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said Vera CPU deployments would include every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab, and system OEM.\n    17\t    Quote: Kress added that the Vera CPU will be deployed by \"every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab, and system OEM.\"\n    18\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2769; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    19\t- e4691 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] NVIDIA expects Vera CPU revenue in FY2028 to be double its FY2027 level.\n    20\t    Quote: What's more, Nvidia expects its Vera CPU revenue to double next year.\n    21\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3660, e4764; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    22\t- e4692 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst), citing Tom's Hardware; original source Tom's Hardware ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] Tom's Hardware reported that Arm-based servers represented 45% of data-center market revenue share in June.\n    23\t    Quote: Tom's Hardware noted in June that Arm-based servers account for 45% of the data center market's revenue share.\n    24\t- e4764 [party_statement; fool.com (Keith Noonan); original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:0ba93692dc5df4b999fe3729 2026-09-11] Jensen Huang forecast that NVIDIA CPU revenue would more than double in FY2028.\n    25\t    Quote: Huang expects CPU revenue to more than double next fiscal year\n    26\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4691; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    27\t- e4856 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:c70bc12967b9b53426b5fb10 2026-09-12] NVIDIA management said Vera CPU revenue in FY2028 could be more than double the $20 billion potential it cited for FY2027.\n    28\t    Quote: with the potential to more than double that number in fiscal year 2028.\n    29\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3660; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    30\t- e4857 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Bank of America analysts ; from gnews:c70bc12967b9b53426b5fb10 2026-09-12] Bank of America analysts forecast that the CPU market would grow fivefold, from $35 billion in 2026 to $170 billion in 2030.\n    31\t    Quote: Analysts at Bank of America also released a research report in June, saying they believe the CPU market will grow fivefold between this year and 2030, from $35 billion to $170 billion.\n    32\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e935; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    33\t- e5044 [party_statement; Insider Monkey; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a1af1bb8840fa4ec9e52bcb0 2026-09-15] NVIDIA said Anthropic is evaluating NVIDIA’s Vera CPU for CPU-intensive agentic workloads.\n    34\t    Quote: Nvidia recently said Anthropic is evaluating its Vera CPU for CPU-intensive agentic workloads,\n    35\t- e5399 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that its 6th Gen EPYC Venice CPUs delivered 20% higher per-core performance than NVIDIA’s 88-core Vera CPU in SPEC 2026.\n    36\t    Quote: Here, the AMD 6th Gen EPYC Venice CPUs provide a 20% uplift over NVIDIA's 88-Core Vera CPU.\n    37\t- e5400 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that its EPYC 9996 delivered 1.2 times Vera-based platform per-core performance and 2.24 times its platform-level performance in SPECrate 2026 Integer testing.\n    38\t    Quote: In SPECrate 2026 Integer testing, the AMD EPYC 9996 server CPU delivers 1.2 times the per-core performance of an Nvidia Vera-based platform and 2.24 times its platform-level performance.\n    39\t- e5401 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD estimated that an EPYC 9996 system in a modeled 100-kilowatt rack would deliver 3.4 times the throughput of a Vera-based platform.\n    40\t    Quote: In a modeled 100-kilowatt rack, it delivers an estimated 3.4 times the throughput of a Vera-based platform.\n    41\t- e5402 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that its 5th Gen EPYC CPUs with 192 cores had a 37% performance gain over NVIDIA Vera, while its 6th Gen EPYC Venice CPU with 256 cores had a 2.37-times gain in the overall SPEC 2026 suite.\n    42\t    Quote: Here, the 5th Gen EPYC CPUs with 192 cores provide a 37% gain over NVIDIA Vera & the 6th Gen EPYC Venice CPU with 256 cores provides a 2.37x gain.\n    43\t- e5403 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that its EPYC 9006 server CPUs averaged 12% higher per-core performance in Agentic AI-specific benchmarks, with a4 subtests averaging 20%.\n    44\t    Quote: Moving over to Agentic AI-specific benchmark tests, AMD's EPYC 9006 Server CPUs drive a 12% gain in per-core performance benchmarks, with the a4 subtests' performance averaging at 20%.\n    45\t- e5404 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that EPYC Venice had 18% faster memory bandwidth than NVIDIA Vera, with 1.6 TB/s versus Vera’s 1.2 TB/s, in STREAM Triad testing.\n    46\t    Quote: AMD's EPYC Venice delivered 18% faster memory bandwidth versus NVIDIA's Vera thanks to its 16-channel memory design, which supports up to 12,800 MT/s RDIMM modules. This provides the platform with 1.6 TB/s of memory bandwidth versus 1.2 TB/s bandwidth on the NVIDIA Vera CPUs (using LPDDR5x).\n    47\t\n    48\t[Themes in force, 10]\n    49\t- v10 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Chinese authorities formally authorize purchases of Nvidia H200 chips by Chinese AI companies by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-09-17; reasons: The reference is rung 2 (No): no market consensus, company guidance, or stated deadline for Chinese authorization is supplied, so the status-quo reference is no authorization by January 31, 2027. The evidence now makes rung 1 more likely: reports describe Chinese approval of H200 purchases for selec\n    50\t- v18 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will the Remote Access Security Act be enacted by December 31, 2026?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-08-19; reasons: The reference is the latest supplied legislative status: the Act passed the House but had not passed the Senate as of August 19, 2026, placing the reference at rung 2 (No); no market-consensus estimate is supplied. With about six weeks remaining at the due date, the implied outcome remains non-enact\n    51\t- v25 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will Taiwan adopt restrictions on AI chip sales to China by December 31, 2026?: now rung 1 No; judged 2026-08-25; reasons: The reference is Taiwan's stated current policy status: no Taiwan-specific restrictions on AI chip sales to China, which is rung 1; no separate market consensus is supplied. The investigation and reports that officials are considering a criminal ban, followed by indictments and seizure of additional\n    52\t- v26 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will Taiwan adopt performance-threshold controls on exports of advanced AI chips by December 31, 2026?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-07-28; reasons: No market consensus or formal target is supplied; the reference is the status-quo baseline of no adopted controls, rung 2. Consultations and active consideration make adoption more plausible than before, but the Ministry confirmed that no timeline is set. With the deadline only five months away, the\n    53\t- v32 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will the Commerce Department announce or adopt restrictions on Chinese AI developers' access to advanced chips through overseas data centers by December 31, 2026?: now rung 1 No; judged 2026-08-19; reasons: The reference is the status quo—no stated market consensus or official timetable—and sits at rung 1. The active review and the reported use of overseas data centers make restrictions more plausible, but the evidence still describes examination and alleged access, not a proposed rule or a dated commi\n    54\t- v40 (ai-capex, due 2026-10-05) Will the relevant regulator approve CME Group’s planned futures contracts tied to H100 and B200 hourly rental costs by October 5, 2026?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-08-29; reasons: The stated plan points to rung 1: CME intends to launch on October 5, conditional on approval. But the evidence in force makes approval by that date less likely than not: the CFTC is still seeking comment on whether the market is sufficiently standardized and transparent, and that comment period run\n    55\t- v49 (semiconductor-cycle, due 2027-02-28) How much will global semiconductor sales total in calendar 2026?: now rung 4 High; judged 2026-09-16; reasons: The market reference is the WSTS and Gartner forecasts, both of which place 2026 sales in rung 4. Their quantified forecasts imply $1.51–$1.56 trillion, within rung 4's $1.50 trillion to below $1.60 trillion range; neither crosses a rung boundary. IDC's 52.8% growth forecast lacks a sales base, so i\n    56\t- v6 (ai-capex, due 2027-02-28) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 4 Upper-middle range; judged 2026-09-19; reasons: The market reference is roughly $720–$745 billion for the four hyperscalers, which sits in rung 4 (e5014). The evidence still implies about $750 billion: the four-company tracking estimate near $745 billion and the separate top-four estimate of $760 billion bracket that level; company capex revision\n    57\t- v7 (ai-capex, due 2028-02-28) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 4 Upper-middle range; judged 2026-09-19; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The market reference is about $1.08 trillion for 2027 hyperscaler capex, which is rung 3. My implied estimate is just above the $1.1 trillion rung-4 boundary: NVIDIA's $1.3 trillion top-five forecast supports a higher spending path, while the four-company, data-center-only scope requires a discount;\n    58\t- v9 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will Congress enact legislation phasing out Chinese-made lidar technology in the United States by December 31, 2026?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-07-07; reasons: The reference is No (rung 2): no market consensus or enacted legislation is supplied, and the stated deadline remains the test. The House committee’s proposal is a concrete step toward enactment, but it is not enactment by Congress; with only this evidence, it raises the chance of Yes without making\n    59\t\n    60\tAnswer per the JSON schema: one calculation for the family and one answer per member.| 39 | 11-05 | 第 3 步：做进正式系统（设计四张表，Codex 审 3 轮：第 1 轮 4 条严重、第 2 轮 2 条严重，都改；第 3 轮无严重问题） | 建成 `baseline.run` + poc-v116 + baseline-v3 提示词文件；$0 核对：44 份请求和验证时逐字节相同，3 次存下的回答走正式代码写出的记录完全一样，第一天判断正好判这些题，网页从开始跟踪那天可见；新测试 12 条、6 个弄坏版本都抓出、全套 272 条通过；设计页和 PRD 00/03/04/05/06 更新，`docs/check.py` 通过 | 交 Alex：要不要跑 14 天三遍完整验收（约 5 美元） |\n| 40 | 11-05 | Alex：\"跑验收\"。14 天三遍完整验收：poc-v116（开始跟踪时读财报）三遍、当天重跑 poc-v108 三遍作对照，冻结尺子打分（事先锁定 `acceptance-design.md`）；电脑中途在电池上睡了约 5 小时，6 遍被暂停，插电后自己接着跑完 | **全过**：v10、v12 各 3/3（对照从没判过）；合计 53 对 45（满分 57，+8）；唯一少两次的 v17（0/3 对 2/3）两人都认定不是起点数：同一份抽取请求，新配置两遍碰巧留下\"中国份额归零\"那句，对照没留；约 4.79 美元 | 交 Alex：poc-v116 是否成为默认 |\n| 41 | 11-05 | Alex：\"都做\"（poc-v116 设成默认；提交代码、开 PR）。默认配置改成 poc-v116，默认的条目数据库换成带财报网页原文的那一份（旧的没有原文，新默认会在它上面拒跑）；测试按用户的用法补上：不给种子题就拒绝，给了就先读财报再跑第一天 | 测试全过，5 个弄坏的版本都抓出；配置的设置和验收三遍记下的校验码一致（只改了配置里的注释）；花钱前的检查脚本在新配置的运行数据上照常跑完，但不复查读财报那一步的引用检查（记为未补的缺口）；D366 | 提交、开 PR；下一类问题等 Alex |\n      control never does counts as \"new noise\"; filed all three times, it counts as \"outright new noise\". The opposite direction offsets it.\n    - Any question with a net 3 fewer, a net 2 lost outright, a net 5 more new noise, or a net 3 more outright new noise fails. These thresholds are the\n      smallest values that keep \"misjudged when new and old are exactly the same\" within about one in ten; at the test, first compute the\n      misjudgement rate from the reassignments; if it is above one in ten, raise the threshold most prone to misjudgement by one step, then compare with\n      what was measured.\n  - **Smaller changes** (a question 2 fewer, two or three more noise) cannot be told from luck in three measurements: all are listed, given to a reader\n    who did not write the rule to read against the raw answers, and written into the report; they do not decide pass or fail.\n  Only when everything passes does it become a candidate; a candidate runs end to end three times on the 14-day window (extraction re-sent, matching and\n  judging run as usual, scored on the frozen judgement ruler), and that step needs Alex's word.\n\n## 7. Process (no drift)\n\n- **One recommendation and one test at a time** (AGENTS §7.3, Alex: \"就测试一种，一次\"): write the design first and put it through rounds of review (own\n  checks, Codex read-only, a reader who did not write it reading the raw records) until a round finds nothing serious; write down and freeze the\n  predictions and stop conditions; test once; if it does not match, stop and take it to Alex (D355: \"如果有什么不符合hypothesis 的地方你需要终止然后和我说我们重新评估\").\n- **A review's report is a lead**: each finding counts only once it is checked against the records (AGENTS §1).\n- **A prompt audit before any prompt change** (`.claude/skills/prompt-audit/SKILL.md`): check the whole request against the standard sentence by\n  sentence, in both directions.\n- **Every step goes into `log.md`**: what was done, what came of it, the files; mistakes and overturned results stay in it too.\n- **Do not repeat what was ruled out** (§3).\n\n## 8. Steps and where we are\n\n| # | Step | Output | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Design: the classes of indirect relation (a closed set + examples from other industries), how to file when a fact measures both sides, deleting the fallback; checked against the standard sentence by sentence; length before and after | `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/design-links.md` | Done (draft 3, v74l / poc-v115) |\n| 2 | Checks at no cost: a sample answer written by the new rule goes through every code check; the new rule's text goes through the prompt audit | Same as above | Done |\n| 3 | Review rounds (Codex, independent readers, own), until nothing serious remains | `review-links-*.md` | Done (Codex 7 rounds, independent readers 3 times; round 7 found nothing serious) |\n| 4 | Draw and freeze the held-out set; write down and freeze this test's predictions and decision rule | `heldout.json`, `predictions-links.md` | Done (sha256 recorded for both) |\n| 5 | Test once: development set + held-out set, the new rule and the same-day control 3 times each | `answers-links.json`, `log.md` | Done (2026-11-04): failed: facts that matter reaching the right question, development set +7 (p 0.26); every other condition passed |\n| 6 | If passed: to Alex, to decide whether to run three 14-day end-to-end draws; if failed: stop, to Alex | `design-links.md` §8 | Stopped, to Alex |\n| 7 | (D361, Alex: \"按照你的提以来，但是你一定是自己反思多轮之后才给出的行动\") Measure the ceiling first: hand the source passages the gold cites straight to the frozen judgement; the custom-chip, operating-margin and capital-spending questions judged 3 times each at the window end, the custom-chip question as it was 3 times as the control; the fallback sentence is not deleted for now | `reflection-v74l.md`, `oracle-design.md` | Done: operating margin grounded 3/3, capital spending 2/3, custom chips 0/3 (the gap is in interpretation); the next step waits for Alex |\n| 8 | (D362, Alex: \"你继续啊不要scope drift and strongly follow evidence of your discovery and learning\") Two small changes where facts are not delivered, one at a time: ① when a quote is rewritten, code matches it back to the source sentence (changing nothing the model sees; verified by replaying the archive); ② a figure question's description states which figures it is derived from by definition (first sent 3 times each on the article that motivated it) | `delivery-fixes.md` | Stopped: an independent check overturned the first change's code (it would take in wrong figures); the second failed its trial send (1/3). The root: the signed standard's §5.3 does not keep two key facts, treating them as \"a restatement of known facts\" (proposal 6, awaiting approval); to Alex |\n| 9 | **Historical; superseded by D370.** (D363, Alex: \"乙\") At coverage start, record each question's starting figure once from the company's latest earnings report; the daily path still takes no restatements: design, rounds of self-checks and independent checks, validated first on the archive in the simplest way | `baseline-design.md` | Validated on two questions: the frozen extraction does not read the report's tables (0/3); with each question reading the report once on its own, the operating-margin question read its starting figure 2/3 and the capital-spending question 3/3, and the judgement then judged right (2/3, 3/3), about 2.5 cents. All 22 questions tested once (2026-11-05, Alex: \"你充分验证\"; files chosen by code, a same-day control, about $0.43): v10 and v12 judged right 9/9 each; no question already judged right turned wrong; but 5 of the 53 figures read were other quantities (the Vera CPU question and the Blackwell and Rubin question took a larger or neighbouring revenue line), so it failed condition 3, written beforehand. The model itself pointed to the reading instructions' sentence \"write the lines it is computed from, or the line the report uses for it\"; stopped, to Alex. Alex: \"改\"; only this sentence was changed (\"write only the lines it is computed from, or the same quantity under another name; never a broader line or a line next to it\"), tried on 4 questions first, then tested on all 22 (about $0.36): all three conditions passed: v10 and v12 9/9 each, no question judged right turned wrong, and all 46 figures were the questions' own quantities. Checked after an independent reader's challenge: the new sentence made the reading miss the capital-spending line in 3 of 12 reads, and it had been tested only on the one May report; the fourth version (a line-by-line re-check after writing) removed too much and failed; after a census of every failure trace (Alex: \"我需要你深度看完所有失败trace给我准信\"), the third version with the repaired quote check was chosen (D365): 0 wrong figures on the held-out August report; built into the production system (poc-v116; the $0 checks and the tests all pass). The full acceptance, three 14-day draws (Alex: \"跑验收\"; a same-day control; about $4.79): all passed: operating margin and capital spending judged right 3/3 each (the control never judged them), 53 against 45 in total (of 57); for the China-share question, the only one two fewer, both readers found the starting figures did not lead it astray. Alex: \"都做\": poc-v116 became the default (D366); the code was committed and a PR opened |\n\n## 9. Alex's own words on this matter (in time order)\n\n- \"你这个单独开一个专题出来解决 … 是promot，是系统结构设计还是到底哪里有问题挂错一堆东西，机制是不是有问题（一定要挂吗？有没有可能是杂音\" (2026-11-03)\n- \"So if it's a customer problem or other companies that you think is relating NVIDIA and it's the wrong evidence, then what is the standard for it? … from a hedge fund analyst standpoint, are these even relevant? Do you think your ruler is wrong in this aspect?\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"你确定下你接下来要改进的量化目标，以及你要看到端到端我们的管线是要做什么的，不要乱硬优化，要看到目标\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"就测试一种，一次 … 能不能自己有个超级自信的定论和证据再给我提建议\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"这些只是类型题，你需要抽象拔高我们现在的核心问题和卡点。这个很重要这样改下去才是对的\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"你把这个写好成下一阶段核心PRD然后不要drift, 把所有需要的context 全部充分记录，每次开始前都需要revisit, 然后开始做\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"go\" (2026-11-04, on the proposed amendments to §5.2 and §6 and on this test)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/judging-v13s.txt; nl -ba docs/DECISIONS-log.md | sed -n '11484,11523p;11559,11591p;11641,11712p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/judging-v13s.txt; nl -ba docs/DECISIONS-log.md | sed -n '11484,11523p;11559,11591p;11641,11712p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\tYou are a senior hedge-fund analyst judging the current rungs of a family of related number theses: one company's quantities that overlap, such as a total and its segment, a level and its growth, or a later year that rests on an earlier year's base. Use only the supplied evidence and themes in force. Each rung is your forecast: the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved.\n     2\t\n     3\t- Judge every member from the evidence shown (what recent judgements relied on and what arrived recently) and the themes in force, as if for the first time. Earlier forecasts are not shown: whether a conclusion changed is compared afterwards.\n     4\t- Judge each member against a reference: what the market expects for that question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on its ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate whose pace you forecast, informed by guidance, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted subject to the evidence requirement below; it remains a forecast when reaffirmed, and is taken in full only when you show what evidence puts the required volume in hand for the period. Build the family's figures once and read every member's outcome from them: a part cannot exceed its whole, a figure that spans several periods or products gives a question only its share, and a quantity the family shares has one forecast. Compare each outcome with its rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\n     5\t- Weigh by tier and by novelty. Only fact can establish that a rung's outcome already holds; \"not established\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. Guidance for the current or next quarter is the strongest forecast input. A management target for a product not yet shipping, or an ambition spanning years, is an input, not an answer: weigh time and execution (how many selling quarters remain, what volume and price the figure implies, what the company's own choices say) under the evidence requirement below; consensus is where the market stands, not the answer either. third_party_view and rumour move the forecast in proportion to their specificity (a number, a date, a named party): a research firm's shipment or revenue tracking with a number counts. A commitment or deal that is announced but not sized is sized from its disclosed comparable (the same programme's other deal, scaled by capacity or units); a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it. A repeat, another outlet for the same claim, or an opinion that adds no new fact or calculation moves nothing. Missing confirmation alone is not grounds for the lowest or highest rung: an event not being confirmed is not confirmation that it did not happen.\n     6\t- An evidence item that replaces another includes its predecessor for context, such as guidance revised from 12% to 25% growth. The predecessor is no longer evidence in force.\n     7\t- \"Themes in force\" lists the library's open theme theses (policy stages, shared quantities) with their current conclusions. They are our own judgements, never evidence: do not cite them in cites. Weigh each one that bears on a member and report it in that member's theme_pushes with the direction it pushes that member (toward_first, toward_last or none); leave out a theme that does not bear on it. A theme marked \"updated since this thesis was last judged\" may change the conclusion; one that is not marked already stood at the previous judgement. When no themes are listed, theme_pushes is empty.\n     8\t- Your conclusions must be consistent with each other and with the themes in force as shown: a share cannot be judged at zero while the policy that gates it is judged likely to open.\n     9\t- Return the family's figures and one answer per member, as the JSON schema defines them. Every number an answer rests on is a figure; a number you judge rather than read or compute is an estimate, stated as yours. Before sizing a share, deduction, discount or pace that decides the band, explain which band the evidence favours. Show support for the size or a justifying bound. If the passages lack the quantity asked, explain why a related figure fits the companies, period and quantity, and why your assumptions favour that band. If you can say only that the outcome might fall short or overshoot, not which band is more likely, answer unresolved and name what is unknown. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\n 11484\t## D745 · 2026-11-23 · The D744 re-read calibrates the readers but stops on a qualifier-list gap and on S's unsupported share; council 59 recommends a targeted $0 test of one clarification, for Alex to authorize\n 11485\t\n 11486\t- **The result** (`docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND3-RESULT.md` §5; `REREAD2-TALLY.json`):\n 11487\t  - **The readers are calibrated.**\n 11488\t    - All five controls passed: midpoint, 108.5 misread, overlap, disjoint, unsupported assumption.\n 11489\t    - No defect was marked by both readers on the 12 replies.\n 11490\t  - **STOP on two lines, as written:**\n 11491\t    1. T10 (S, Claude writer) F11: \"an estimated $750 billion\" recorded with qualifier \"none\". Both adjudicators upheld it. \"estimated\" is\n 11492\t       not in the closed qualifier list, so the format forced \"none\". This is 1 affected figure of 251 source figures in the three writer\n 11493\t       runs.\n 11494\t    2. S has zero usable replies. Both writers state a data-center share of total capex (90%; 95–100%) that the passages do not size, and\n 11495\t       the share decides the band.\n 11496\t  - **E's Codex reply** is now \"not supported\": its 15% cut from the guided floor decides the band. This is the same weakness.\n 11497\t- **Council round 59** (`codex-r59.md`) chose a targeted $0 test, (c), over Claude's lean to go to Alex for the paid re-send, (b):\n 11498\t  - Going straight to the paid re-send would measure the known weakness in gpt-6-luna without the request saying what a supported size\n 11499\t    is.\n 11500\t  - Skipping a clean result needs Alex to change the registered sequence anyway.\n 11501\t  - Claude agrees: the weakness is unsupported precision in a number that decides the rung, which is the goal's own failure (D725).\n 11502\t- **Proposed test (for Alex to authorize):**\n 11503\t  1. Add \"estimated\" to the qualifier list, and show readers the allowed values. Prove it on the saved replies, with a case and a\n 11504\t     mutant.\n 11505\t  2. Add one clarification to the existing instruction: when a deduction or cut decides the band, show the evidence for its size or a\n 11506\t     bound the passages support that justifies the band; if neither, answer unresolved.\n 11507\t  3. Test on S and on one stored case where the passages do support a narrower figure.\n 11508\t  4. Before the test, Alex rules whether a justified unresolved answer counts as usable.\n 11509\t  - Falsifier: a fresh S answer still picks a band on an unsupported share, or the supported case is withheld.\n 11510\t- **Alex's answers** (2026-11-23):\n 11511\t  - to \"怎么走？\": \"批准针对性小测 (推荐)\". The other options were \"不小测，直接议花钱重发\" and \"先停在这里\".\n 11512\t  - to whether a justified \"无法判断\" counts: \"算合格 (推荐)\". The other option was \"不算\".\n 11513\t- **Decided (Alex):**\n 11514\t  1. Run the targeted $0 test.\n 11515\t  2. An unresolved answer whose reason shows the passages cannot settle the rung counts as a usable answer.\n 11516\t- **Council round 60** (`codex-r60.md`) set the wording and the registration (`SMALL-DESIGN.md` §5 [D745]):\n 11517\t  - Rung-deciding shares, deductions and discounts must show their passage support or a justifying bound, else unresolved.\n 11518\t  - The default discount is made subject to that rule.\n 11519\t  - The three controls are the saved E 15% cut, a vague P-v8 unresolved answer and a justified S unresolved answer (expected no, no,\n 11520\t    yes).\n 11521\t  - Pass: every S and E reply settled yes on a forecast or its reason; both P-v8 replies settled yes on a forecast. A support split\n 11522\t    is inconclusive.\n 11523\t\n 11559\t    is more likely.\n 11560\t  - **The one test after his ruling.** A $0 blinded re-read of nine stored answers under his words, added to the shared definitions:\n 11561\t    - the answers: U01–U06, U08, the third run's T10 (for its spending argument only), and T15 (the accepted E bracket);\n 11562\t    - fresh readers, one per family;\n 11563\t    - what his choice implies for each answer is recorded before reading.\n 11564\t    - It moves on to a writer repair only if both families apply his words alike. A continuing split leaves the publication question\n 11565\t      open in practice. A clean re-read does not turn D745 into a pass.\n 11566\t  - **The paid re-send stays on hold** until an E writer repair passes a clean $0 test. The proposed repair: the writer chooses between\n 11567\t    a forecast and \"can't tell\" before it assigns a discount, naming what makes one side more likely under its own assumptions. It is a\n 11568\t    new arm and goes to council before any send. Publishing management's figure whenever it sits on a cutoff would change the product,\n 11569\t    and needs Alex's own decision.\n 11570\t- **The question to Alex** (pending): \"资料没直接给出所问的数。用一个相关数字作比较，能解释为什么结果更可能在线的这一边，但另一边仍有可能。这时可以发布预测，还是要等到能从资料算出结果落在线的哪一边？\"\n 11571\t- **Not done.** Nothing was paid. Nothing was tuned on the 36 stored answers. The request was not changed after the registration.\n 11572\t\n 11573\t## D747 · 2026-11-23 · Alex rules that a forecast may rest on an explained indirect comparison\n 11574\t\n 11575\t- **The question** (after D746): \"资料没直接给出题目问的那个数，只能拿一个相关的数字来比。比如问四大云厂商今年的数据中心开支能不能过 7000 亿，资料只说“这四家 AI 基础设施开支超过 7300 亿”。这种比较能说明结果更可能落在线的某一边，但另一边也还有可能。这时系统可以发布预测吗，还是要等资料能直接算出落在哪边？\"\n 11576\t  - Options:\n 11577\t    - \"可以发布，要讲清楚 (推荐)\": the answer must explain why the comparison applies to the companies, the year and the quantity\n 11578\t      asked, and why, under its own assumptions, that side is more likely; an answer that cannot explain it is not supported;\n 11579\t    - \"先说“还判断不了”\";\n 11580\t    - \"先停在这里\".\n 11581\t- **Alex's answer** (2026-11-23): \"可以发布，要讲清楚 (推荐)\".\n 11582\t- **Decided (Alex).** When the passages do not give the asked quantity, a forecast may rest on a related figure. It is supported\n 11583\t  only when the answer explains two things: why the comparison applies to the companies, the period and the quantity asked; and why,\n 11584\t  under the answer's own stated assumptions, the chosen side of the line is more likely. An answer that cannot explain this is not\n 11585\t  supported.\n 11586\t  - D741 ruling 3 still holds: a total used for a narrower quantity states the deduction as its own figure, or gives a supported\n 11587\t    bound.\n 11588\t  - D745's ruling still holds: a justified unresolved answer counts as usable.\n 11589\t- **Next (D746).** The ruling's words go into the shared reader definitions (`blind_rules.py`). Then one $0 blinded re-read of nine\n 11590\t  stored answers, registered with its expected readings and reviewed by the council before any packet is read.\n 11591\t\n 11641\t## D749 · 2026-11-23 · Alex authorizes the E writer repair and its $0 writer test\n 11642\t\n 11643\t- **The question** (after D748): \"下一步怎么走？\"\n 11644\t  - Options:\n 11645\t    - \"批准修写手再小测 (推荐)\": the writer first decides whether the evidence favours one side of the line, then whether to discount;\n 11646\t      if it can only say sales might fall short, it answers \"can't tell\" and says what is missing. The changed request goes to the\n 11647\t      council, then one $0 writer test on E, S and P with the two failed answers as controls. A clean pass comes back to Alex for the\n 11648\t      paid re-send.\n 11649\t    - \"不修，直接议花钱重发\".\n 11650\t    - \"先停在这里\".\n 11651\t- **Alex's answer** (2026-11-23): \"批准修写手再小测 (推荐)\".\n 11652\t- **Decided (Alex).** Repair the writer request as council 63 described. Review it in council before any send. Then run one $0\n 11653\t  blind writer test on E, S and P-v8, with the saved U05 (invented cut) and U02 (impossible floor) as reader controls, under D748's\n 11654\t  pass lines. The paid re-send stays on hold until that test passes clean.\n 11655\t\n 11656\t## D750 · 2026-11-23 · The D749 writer test is INCONCLUSIVE with no failed line; council 65 (under D439): one bullet-2 sentence on reaffirmed targets, then a second $0 writer test\n 11657\t\n 11658\t- **Alex's direction** (2026-11-23, before sleeping): \"你接下来开始自行收敛，方向吧，我要准备睡了，所以这边的决策交给你，还有 Council， 记得不要 Scope Drift， 我们的目标是把剩下有问题的地方收敛起来，然后准备要往 Production 方向去走了，所以你自己每次复习的时候看 Log， 看我们试过了哪些东西，不要犯一模一样的错误，然后赶紧的往收敛方向去做测量和把它定下来。\"\n 11659\t  - D439 governs: the council decides reversibly, no paid send (its ceiling is spent), nothing committed.\n 11660\t- **The result** (`docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND5-RESULT.md`; run `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/`):\n 11661\t  - **The repair worked where it was aimed.**\n 11662\t    - E's Codex writer, which invented a cut in both earlier runs, now answers a justified unresolved, accepted by both families.\n 11663\t    - Both P-v8 replies are supported forecasts.\n 11664\t    - Both controls were caught: the invented cut settled no, and the impossible floor was upheld by both adjudicators.\n 11665\t    - No fresh defect, no refusal. S's Codex forecast is supported.\n 11666\t  - **Two splits:**\n 11667\t    - X05, E Claude, takes a not-yet-shipping target in full, asserting \"already selling at volume\". No passage shows shipments,\n 11668\t      revenue to date or orders.\n 11669\t    - X08, S Claude: one reader cites \"more than $730B\" as a floor above its own $710.4B outcome, the other reads it as a guide.\n 11670\t- **Claude's error, corrected.** I first recorded X08's reasons as compatible although my own note named the tension. Council 65\n 11671\t  caught it. X08 was then adjudicated as registered and stayed split.\n 11672\t- **Council round 65** (`claude-r65.md`, `codex-r65.md`) reached consensus:\n 11673\t  - X05's premise is a question of fact under the existing rule, not a gap in Alex's words.\n 11674\t  - Claude's closed list of \"volume in hand\" was rejected: it was wider and narrower than the standard at once.\n 11675\t  - The smaller sentence is at bullet 2: \"it remains a forecast when reaffirmed, and is taken in full only when you show what evidence\n 11676\t    puts the required volume in hand for the period\".\n 11677\t  - One $0 writer test on all three cases (Alex's three-case condition), with the same controls and lines. On S, a reading that cites a\n 11678\t    floor above the answer's own outcome is not compatible with one that reads a guide.\n 11679\t  - The paid re-send stays on hold.\n 11680\t- **Done.** The sentence was changed (4,794 → 4,867 characters). Cases 111 of 111, rehearsal 19 of 19, pinned plan unchanged. The test\n 11681\t  is registered (`SMALL-DESIGN.md` §5 [D750]) and built by `blind6.py`. Council round 66 reviews it before any send.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'for d in poc/data/judging-v13s-blind{3,4,5,6}-*/E; do wc -lc \"$d\"/answers/*.json; shasum \"$d/user.txt\"; done\nnl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-*/E/user.txt | head -n 34\nfor f in poc/data/judging-v13s-blind{3,4,5,6}-*/E/answers/*.json; do echo \"$f\"; cat \"$f\"; done'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'for d in poc/data/judging-v13s-blind{3,4,5,6}-*/E; do wc -lc \"$d\"/answers/*.json; shasum \"$d/user.txt\"; done\nnl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-*/E/user.txt | head -n 34\nfor f in poc/data/judging-v13s-blind{3,4,5,6}-*/E/answers/*.json; do echo \"$f\"; cat \"$f\"; done'","aggregated_output":"     188    8447 poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/answers/claude.json\n     203    6544 poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/answers/codex.json\n     391   14991 total\n5c123d4f2ade8bb7f36123109f4d03111dfffd2c  poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/user.txt\n     188    9740 poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json\n     170    4951 poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json\n     358   14691 total\n5c123d4f2ade8bb7f36123109f4d03111dfffd2c  poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/user.txt\n       1    6687 poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json\n      92    4067 poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json\n      93   10754 total\n5c123d4f2ade8bb7f36123109f4d03111dfffd2c  poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/E/user.txt\n       1    7998 poc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json\n     107    4052 poc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json\n     108   12050 total\n5c123d4f2ade8bb7f36123109f4d03111dfffd2c  poc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23/E/user.txt\n     1\t[Family] members:\n     2\tThesis v41 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA Vera CPU revenue\", FY2028, due 2028-02-29): What will NVIDIA report as Vera CPU revenue for FY2028?\n     3\tLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $50 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $40 billion and less than $50 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $30 billion and less than $40 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $20 billion and less than $30 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is less than $20 billion.\n     4\t\n     5\tRelations: none.\n     6\tShared quantities, one forecast figure each; on every arithmetic path from an outcome, through rates and multiples too, the first figure of that quantity reached is its forecast: none.\n     7\tOutcome keys (population | measure | scope | period), in USD bn, to be used exactly: v41 = NVIDIA | revenue | Vera CPU | FY2028.\n     8\t\n     9\t[Evidence in view for the family, 15 items]\n    10\t- e3660 [party_statement; Bram Berkowitz, fool.com, reporting Colette Kress's statement; original source NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress ; from gnews:fd25a8ea6f60290264bfbe55 2026-08-30] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said NVIDIA still expects $20 billion of CPU revenue in FY2027 and expects FY2028 CPU revenue to more than double that amount.\n    11\t    Quote: The bad news for AMD and Intel is that Kress said Nvidia not only still expects $20 billion in CPU revenue in its fiscal 2027, but expects that number to more than double in fiscal 2028.\n    12\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3302, e4691, e4856; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    13\t- e4687 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source AMD ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] AMD estimates that server CPU market revenue could grow at a 50% annual rate through 2030, reaching a $220 billion opportunity by the end of the decade.\n    14\t    Quote: AMD notes that the server CPU market's revenue could increase at an annual rate of 50% through 2030, creating a $220 billion revenue opportunity by the end of the decade.\n    15\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4084; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    16\t- e4689 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst), quoting Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said Vera CPU deployments would include every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab, and system OEM.\n    17\t    Quote: Kress added that the Vera CPU will be deployed by \"every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab, and system OEM.\"\n    18\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2769; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    19\t- e4691 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] NVIDIA expects Vera CPU revenue in FY2028 to be double its FY2027 level.\n    20\t    Quote: What's more, Nvidia expects its Vera CPU revenue to double next year.\n    21\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3660, e4764; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    22\t- e4692 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst), citing Tom's Hardware; original source Tom's Hardware ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] Tom's Hardware reported that Arm-based servers represented 45% of data-center market revenue share in June.\n    23\t    Quote: Tom's Hardware noted in June that Arm-based servers account for 45% of the data center market's revenue share.\n    24\t- e4764 [party_statement; fool.com (Keith Noonan); original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:0ba93692dc5df4b999fe3729 2026-09-11] Jensen Huang forecast that NVIDIA CPU revenue would more than double in FY2028.\n    25\t    Quote: Huang expects CPU revenue to more than double next fiscal year\n    26\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4691; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    27\t- e4856 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:c70bc12967b9b53426b5fb10 2026-09-12] NVIDIA management said Vera CPU revenue in FY2028 could be more than double the $20 billion potential it cited for FY2027.\n    28\t    Quote: with the potential to more than double that number in fiscal year 2028.\n    29\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3660; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    30\t- e4857 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Bank of America analysts ; from gnews:c70bc12967b9b53426b5fb10 2026-09-12] Bank of America analysts forecast that the CPU market would grow fivefold, from $35 billion in 2026 to $170 billion in 2030.\n    31\t    Quote: Analysts at Bank of America also released a research report in June, saying they believe the CPU market will grow fivefold between this year and 2030, from $35 billion to $170 billion.\n    32\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e935; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    33\t- e5044 [party_statement; Insider Monkey; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a1af1bb8840fa4ec9e52bcb0 2026-09-15] NVIDIA said Anthropic is evaluating NVIDIA’s Vera CPU for CPU-intensive agentic workloads.\n    34\t    Quote: Nvidia recently said Anthropic is evaluating its Vera CPU for CPU-intensive agentic workloads,\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/answers/claude.json\n{\n  \"figures\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F1\",\n      \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"scope\": \"Vera CPU\", \"period\": \"FY2027\"},\n      \"kind\": \"source\",\n      \"basis\": \"guidance\",\n      \"value\": 20,\n      \"low\": null,\n      \"high\": null,\n      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e3660\",\n      \"quote_number\": \"$20 billion\",\n      \"qualifier\": \"none\",\n      \"inputs\": null,\n      \"operation\": null,\n      \"basis_text\": null,\n      \"rests_on\": null,\n      \"outcome_for\": [],\n      \"reference_for\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F2\",\n      \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue growth multiple\", \"scope\": \"Vera CPU\", \"period\": \"FY2028\"},\n      \"kind\": \"source\",\n      \"basis\": \"guidance\",\n      \"value\": 2,\n      \"low\": null,\n      \"high\": null,\n      \"unit\": \"x\",\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e4856\",\n      \"quote_number\": \"double\",\n      \"qualifier\": \"more than\",\n      \"inputs\": null,\n      \"operation\": null,\n      \"basis_text\": null,\n      \"rests_on\": null,\n      \"outcome_for\": [],\n      \"reference_for\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F3\",\n      \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"scope\": \"Vera CPU\", \"period\": \"FY2028\"},\n      \"kind\": \"computed\",\n      \"basis\": \"guidance\",\n      \"value\": 40,\n      \"low\": null,\n      \"high\": null,\n      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n      \"evidence_id\": null,\n      \"quote_number\": null,\n      \"qualifier\": null,\n      \"inputs\": [\"F1\", \"F2\"],\n      \"operation\": \"product\",\n      \"basis_text\": null,\n      \"rests_on\": null,\n      \"outcome_for\": [],\n      \"reference_for\": [\"v41\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F4\",\n      \"what\": {\"population\": \"server CPU market\", \"measure\": \"revenue growth\", \"scope\": \"server CPU\", \"period\": \"annual rate through CY2030\"},\n      \"kind\": \"source\",\n      \"basis\": \"third_party\",\n      \"value\": 50,\n      \"low\": null,\n      \"high\": null,\n      \"unit\": \"%\",\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e4687\",\n      \"quote_number\": \"50%\",\n      \"qualifier\": \"none\",\n      \"inputs\": null,\n      \"operation\": null,\n      \"basis_text\": null,\n      \"rests_on\": null,\n      \"outcome_for\": [],\n      \"reference_for\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F5\",\n      \"what\": {\"population\": \"CPU market\", \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"scope\": \"CPU\", \"period\": \"CY2026\"},\n      \"kind\": \"source\",\n      \"basis\": \"third_party\",\n      \"value\": 35,\n      \"low\": null,\n      \"high\": null,\n      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e4857\",\n      \"quote_number\": \"$35 billion\",\n      \"qualifier\": \"none\",\n      \"inputs\": null,\n      \"operation\": null,\n      \"basis_text\": null,\n      \"rests_on\": null,\n      \"outcome_for\": [],\n      \"reference_for\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F6\",\n      \"what\": {\"population\": \"CPU market\", \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"scope\": \"CPU\", \"period\": \"CY2030\"},\n      \"kind\": \"source\",\n      \"basis\": \"third_party\",\n      \"value\": 170,\n      \"low\": null,\n      \"high\": null,\n      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e4857\",\n      \"quote_number\": \"$170 billion\",\n      \"qualifier\": \"none\",\n      \"inputs\": null,\n      \"operation\": null,\n      \"basis_text\": null,\n      \"rests_on\": null,\n      \"outcome_for\": [],\n      \"reference_for\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F7\",\n      \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue growth\", \"scope\": \"Vera CPU\", \"period\": \"FY2028\"},\n      \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n      \"basis\": \"forecast\",\n      \"value\": 85,\n      \"low\": null,\n      \"high\": null,\n      \"unit\": \"%\",\n      \"evidence_id\": null,\n      \"quote_number\": null,\n      \"qualifier\": null,\n      \"inputs\": null,\n      \"operation\": null,\n      \"basis_text\": \"My estimate of FY2028 Vera CPU revenue growth on the FY2027 base. Management's 'more than double' (F2, at least 100% growth) is the benchmark, but it is a target for a fiscal year that has not started, for a product line still ramping, so it is discounted for time and execution rather than taken in full. Outside views put CPU market growth near 50% a year (AMD, F4; Bank of America's fivefold path from F5 to F6), so a doubling needs large share gains. Broad announced adoption (every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab and OEM, e4689; Anthropic evaluating Vera, e5044; Arm servers' large revenue share, e4692) supports a pace well above the market, while AMD's own benchmark claims against Vera (e5399, e5400, e5401, e5402, e5404) show standalone CPU share will be contested. Weighting the company target most heavily, tempered toward the market pace, gives 85%.\",\n      \"rests_on\": [\"F2\", \"F4\", \"F5\", \"F6\", \"e4689\", \"e5044\", \"e4692\", \"e5399\", \"e5400\", \"e5401\", \"e5402\", \"e5404\"],\n      \"outcome_for\": [],\n      \"reference_for\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F8\",\n      \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"scope\": \"Vera CPU\", \"period\": \"FY2028\"},\n      \"kind\": \"computed\",\n      \"basis\": \"forecast\",\n      \"value\": 37,\n      \"low\": null,\n      \"high\": null,\n      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n      \"evidence_id\": null,\n      \"quote_number\": null,\n      \"qualifier\": null,\n      \"inputs\": [\"F1\", \"F7\"],\n      \"operation\": \"apply_growth\",\n      \"basis_text\": null,\n      \"rests_on\": null,\n      \"outcome_for\": [\"v41\"],\n      \"reference_for\": []\n    }\n  ],\n  \"answers\": [\n    {\n      \"view_id\": \"v41\",\n      \"shape\": \"forecast\",\n      \"rung\": 3,\n      \"reference_unavailable\": null,\n      \"reasons\": \"Reference: NVIDIA's own guidance. CFO Colette Kress reaffirmed about $20 billion of CPU revenue for FY2027 (F1, e3660) and said FY2028 would more than double it (F2, e4856); NVIDIA's wording ties both the base and the doubling to Vera CPU revenue (e4691, e4856). That gives more than $40 billion (F3), the bottom edge of rung 2 (Bullish, $40-50 billion). Forecast: this is a target for a full fiscal year that has not yet started, for a product line still ramping, so it is discounted for time and execution rather than taken in full; the whole of FY2028 is still to be sold. Outside benchmarks put CPU market growth near 50% a year (AMD's server CPU estimate, F4; Bank of America's path from $35 billion to $170 billion, F5 and F6), roughly half the pace NVIDIA needs, so a doubling requires large share gains. Adoption breadth supports a pace well above the market: Kress says every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab and system OEM will deploy Vera (e4689), Anthropic is evaluating it for CPU-heavy agentic work (e5044), and Arm-based servers already hold a large share of data-center revenue (e4692). Against that, AMD's own benchmarks claim its Venice and EPYC 9996 parts beat Vera per core, per platform, per rack and in memory bandwidth (e5399, e5400, e5401, e5402, e5404); these are a competitor's marketing tests from one article and are weighed lightly, but they show standalone CPU share will be contested. The CEO's and other outlets' repeats of the doubling (e4764, e4691, e4856) add no new fact. The 2027 hyperscaler capex theme, upgraded to the upper-middle range, supports strong AI infrastructure demand in the year that matters but does not by itself imply a doubling of CPU revenue. I forecast 85% growth (F7) on the $20 billion base, giving $37 billion (F8): rung 3 (Neutral), just below the rung-2 line. It would move to rung 2 if FY2027 lands at or above $20 billion with Vera Rubin shipping on schedule, or if NVIDIA sizes FY2028 Vera orders; a confirmed Rubin delay or an FY2027 shortfall would push it toward rung 4.\",\n      \"why\": null,\n      \"cites\": [\"e3660\", \"e4691\", \"e4764\", \"e4856\", \"e4687\", \"e4857\", \"e4689\", \"e4692\", \"e5044\", \"e5399\", \"e5400\", \"e5401\", \"e5402\", \"e5404\"],\n      \"pushes\": [\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e3660\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4691\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4764\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4856\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4687\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4857\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4689\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4692\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5044\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5399\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5400\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5401\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5402\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5403\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5404\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}\n      ],\n      \"theme_pushes\": [\n        {\"view_id\": \"v7\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n        {\"view_id\": \"v6\", \"push\": \"none\"}\n      ]\n    }\n  ]\n}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/answers/codex.json\n{\n  \"figures\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F1\",\n      \"what\": {\n        \"population\": \"NVIDIA\",\n        \"measure\": \"revenue\",\n        \"scope\": \"CPU\",\n        \"period\": \"FY2027\"\n      },\n      \"kind\": \"source\",\n      \"basis\": \"guidance\",\n      \"value\": 20,\n      \"low\": null,\n      \"high\": null,\n      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e3660\",\n      \"quote_number\": \"$20 billion\",\n      \"qualifier\": \"none\",\n      \"inputs\": null,\n      \"operation\": null,\n      \"basis_text\": null,\n      \"rests_on\": null,\n      \"outcome_for\": [],\n      \"reference_for\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F2\",\n      \"what\": {\n        \"population\": \"NVIDIA\",\n        \"measure\": \"revenue multiple\",\n        \"scope\": \"FY2028 Vera CPU revenue relative to the FY2027 CPU outlook\",\n        \"period\": \"FY2028\"\n      },\n      \"kind\": \"source\",\n      \"basis\": \"guidance\",\n      \"value\": 2,\n      \"low\": null,\n      \"high\": null,\n      \"unit\": \"x\",\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e4856\",\n      \"quote_number\": \"double\",\n      \"qualifier\": \"more than\",\n      \"inputs\": null,\n      \"operation\": null,\n      \"basis_text\": null,\n      \"rests_on\": null,\n      \"outcome_for\": [],\n      \"reference_for\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F3\",\n      \"what\": {\n        \"population\": \"NVIDIA\",\n        \"measure\": \"revenue\",\n        \"scope\": \"Vera CPU\",\n        \"period\": \"FY2028\"\n      },\n      \"kind\": \"computed\",\n      \"basis\": \"guidance\",\n      \"value\": 40,\n      \"low\": null,\n      \"high\": null,\n      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n      \"evidence_id\": null,\n      \"quote_number\": null,\n      \"qualifier\": null,\n      \"inputs\": [\"F1\", \"F2\"],\n      \"operation\": \"product\",\n      \"basis_text\": null,\n      \"rests_on\": null,\n      \"outcome_for\": [],\n      \"reference_for\": [\"v41\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F4\",\n      \"what\": {\n        \"population\": \"NVIDIA\",\n        \"measure\": \"revenue realization share\",\n        \"scope\": \"Vera CPU revenue as a share of the stated CPU opportunity\",\n        \"period\": \"FY2027\"\n      },\n      \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n      \"basis\": \"forecast\",\n      \"value\": 85,\n      \"low\": null,\n      \"high\": null,\n      \"unit\": \"%\",\n      \"evidence_id\": null,\n      \"quote_number\": null,\n      \"qualifier\": null,\n      \"inputs\": null,\n      \"operation\": null,\n      \"basis_text\": \"I estimate that Vera realizes most of the stated CPU opportunity in its initial ramp. Expected deployment across major customers supports a high share, while the evidence gives no sales volumes to justify assigning the entire CPU opportunity to realized Vera revenue.\",\n      \"rests_on\": [\"F1\", \"e4689\", \"e5044\"],\n      \"outcome_for\": [],\n      \"reference_for\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F5\",\n      \"what\": {\n        \"population\": \"NVIDIA\",\n        \"measure\": \"revenue\",\n        \"scope\": \"Vera CPU\",\n        \"period\": \"FY2027\"\n      },\n      \"kind\": \"computed\",\n      \"basis\": \"forecast\",\n      \"value\": 17,\n      \"low\": null,\n      \"high\": null,\n      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n      \"evidence_id\": null,\n      \"quote_number\": null,\n      \"qualifier\": null,\n      \"inputs\": [\"F1\", \"F4\"],\n      \"operation\": \"share\",\n      \"basis_text\": null,\n      \"rests_on\": null,\n      \"outcome_for\": [],\n      \"reference_for\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F6\",\n      \"what\": {\n        \"population\": \"NVIDIA\",\n        \"measure\": \"revenue multiple\",\n        \"scope\": \"Vera CPU FY2028 versus FY2027\",\n        \"period\": \"FY2028\"\n      },\n      \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n      \"basis\": \"forecast\",\n      \"value\": 2,\n      \"low\": null,\n      \"high\": null,\n      \"unit\": \"x\",\n      \"evidence_id\": null,\n      \"quote_number\": null,\n      \"qualifier\": null,\n      \"inputs\": null,\n      \"operation\": null,\n      \"basis_text\": \"I estimate that Vera revenue doubles from the discounted launch-year base as deployments broaden. Management's expansion outlook sets the benchmark for the pace. Anthropic is evaluating Vera, and AMD's platform and agentic-workload benchmarks suggest competition for additional deployments.\",\n      \"rests_on\": [\"F2\", \"F5\", \"e4689\", \"e4691\", \"e5044\", \"e5400\", \"e5403\"],\n      \"outcome_for\": [],\n      \"reference_for\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F7\",\n      \"what\": {\n        \"population\": \"NVIDIA\",\n        \"measure\": \"revenue\",\n        \"scope\": \"Vera CPU\",\n        \"period\": \"FY2028\"\n      },\n      \"kind\": \"computed\",\n      \"basis\": \"forecast\",\n      \"value\": 34,\n      \"low\": null,\n      \"high\": null,\n      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n      \"evidence_id\": null,\n      \"quote_number\": null,\n      \"qualifier\": null,\n      \"inputs\": [\"F5\", \"F6\"],\n      \"operation\": \"product\",\n      \"basis_text\": null,\n      \"rests_on\": null,\n      \"outcome_for\": [\"v41\"],\n      \"reference_for\": []\n    }\n  ],\n  \"answers\": [\n    {\n      \"view_id\": \"v41\",\n      \"shape\": \"forecast\",\n      \"rung\": 3,\n      \"reference_unavailable\": null,\n      \"reasons\": \"Management's reference starts at $40 billion, the lower edge of rung 2. Its more-than-double language points above that threshold without specifying by how much. My forecast is $34 billion, in rung 3. I estimate $17 billion of FY2027 Vera revenue from the $20 billion CPU outlook and expect Vera revenue to double as deployments broaden. NVIDIA expects broad deployment, and Anthropic's evaluation is a concrete sign of interest. Neither statement gives committed purchase volumes. AMD's own tests suggest that Vera faces competition for CPU-intensive workloads, with an uncertain effect on sales. The hyperscaler spending outlook, especially the updated calendar 2027 assessment, supports the ramp, but gives no Vera allocation.\",\n      \"why\": null,\n      \"cites\": [\"e3660\", \"e4856\", \"e4689\", \"e4691\", \"e5044\", \"e5400\", \"e5403\"],\n      \"pushes\": [\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e3660\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4687\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4689\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4691\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4692\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4764\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4856\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4857\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5044\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5399\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5400\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5401\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5402\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5403\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5404\", \"push\": \"none\"}\n      ],\n      \"theme_pushes\": [\n        {\"view_id\": \"v6\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n        {\"view_id\": \"v7\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"}\n      ]\n    }\n  ]\n}poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json\n{\n  \"figures\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F1\",\n      \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"scope\": \"CPU\", \"period\": \"FY2027\"},\n      \"kind\": \"source\",\n      \"basis\": \"guidance\",\n      \"value\": 20,\n      \"low\": null,\n      \"high\": null,\n      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e3660\",\n      \"quote_number\": \"$20 billion\",\n      \"qualifier\": \"none\",\n      \"inputs\": null,\n      \"operation\": null,\n      \"basis_text\": null,\n      \"rests_on\": null,\n      \"outcome_for\": [],\n      \"reference_for\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F2\",\n      \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue multiple over prior year\", \"scope\": \"CPU\", \"period\": \"FY2028\"},\n      \"kind\": \"source\",\n      \"basis\": \"guidance\",\n      \"value\": 2,\n      \"low\": null,\n      \"high\": null,\n      \"unit\": \"x\",\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e3660\",\n      \"quote_number\": \"double\",\n      \"qualifier\": \"more than\",\n      \"inputs\": null,\n      \"operation\": null,\n      \"basis_text\": null,\n      \"rests_on\": null,\n      \"outcome_for\": [],\n      \"reference_for\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F3\",\n      \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"scope\": \"CPU\", \"period\": \"FY2028\"},\n      \"kind\": \"computed\",\n      \"basis\": \"guidance\",\n      \"value\": 40,\n      \"low\": null,\n      \"high\": null,\n      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n      \"evidence_id\": null,\n      \"quote_number\": null,\n      \"qualifier\": null,\n      \"inputs\": [\"F1\", \"F2\"],\n      \"operation\": \"product\",\n      \"basis_text\": null,\n      \"rests_on\": null,\n      \"outcome_for\": [],\n      \"reference_for\": [\"v41\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F4\",\n      \"what\": {\"population\": \"Server CPU market\", \"measure\": \"annual revenue growth\", \"scope\": \"all vendors\", \"period\": \"CY2026-CY2030\"},\n      \"kind\": \"source\",\n      \"basis\": \"third_party\",\n      \"value\": 50,\n      \"low\": null,\n      \"high\": null,\n      \"unit\": \"%\",\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e4687\",\n      \"quote_number\": \"50%\",\n      \"qualifier\": \"none\",\n      \"inputs\": null,\n      \"operation\": null,\n      \"basis_text\": null,\n      \"rests_on\": null,\n      \"outcome_for\": [],\n      \"reference_for\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F5\",\n      \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue multiple over prior year\", \"scope\": \"CPU\", \"period\": \"FY2028\"},\n      \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n      \"basis\": \"forecast\",\n      \"value\": 1.8,\n      \"low\": null,\n      \"high\": null,\n      \"unit\": \"x\",\n      \"evidence_id\": null,\n      \"quote_number\": null,\n      \"qualifier\": null,\n      \"inputs\": null,\n      \"operation\": null,\n      \"basis_text\": \"My forecast of FY2028 CPU revenue growth over FY2027 is 1.8x. Management's outlook of more than 2x (F2) is a full-year target for the next fiscal year: all four selling quarters are still ahead, and it rests on a Vera ramp that no passage yet shows in volume. So it is discounted for time and execution. Anthropic is only evaluating Vera (e5044), deployments are described in the future tense (e4689), and AMD claims its EPYC parts beat Vera by 1.2x per core up to 3.4x per modeled rack (e5399-e5404, a competitor's own tests, weighed lightly as one signal). The lower anchor is the outside market pace of about 50% a year (F4; BofA's fivefold growth from 2026 to 2030 in e4857 is a similar pace), which is what a vendor that only holds its share would reach (1.5x). NVIDIA's claim of deployment at every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab and OEM (e4689) and Arm's 45% revenue share of data-center servers (e4692) support gaining share above that pace. 1.8x sits between the market pace (1.5x) and the guidance floor (2.0x), weighted toward the guidance because the CFO reaffirmed it.\",\n      \"rests_on\": [\"F2\", \"F4\", \"e3660\", \"e4689\", \"e4692\", \"e4857\", \"e5044\", \"e5399\", \"e5400\", \"e5401\", \"e5402\", \"e5403\", \"e5404\"],\n      \"outcome_for\": [],\n      \"reference_for\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F6\",\n      \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"scope\": \"CPU\", \"period\": \"FY2028\"},\n      \"kind\": \"computed\",\n      \"basis\": \"forecast\",\n      \"value\": 36,\n      \"low\": null,\n      \"high\": null,\n      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n      \"evidence_id\": null,\n      \"quote_number\": null,\n      \"qualifier\": null,\n      \"inputs\": [\"F1\", \"F5\"],\n      \"operation\": \"product\",\n      \"basis_text\": null,\n      \"rests_on\": null,\n      \"outcome_for\": [],\n      \"reference_for\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F7\",\n      \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"share of CPU revenue\", \"scope\": \"Vera CPU\", \"period\": \"FY2028\"},\n      \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n      \"basis\": \"forecast\",\n      \"value\": 90,\n      \"low\": null,\n      \"high\": null,\n      \"unit\": \"%\",\n      \"evidence_id\": null,\n      \"quote_number\": null,\n      \"qualifier\": null,\n      \"inputs\": null,\n      \"operation\": null,\n      \"basis_text\": \"I estimate Vera at 90% of NVIDIA's FY2028 CPU revenue. Management attributes the FY2028 doubling to Vera itself (e4691: 'Nvidia expects its Vera CPU revenue to double next year'; e4856), and Vera is to be deployed by every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab and system OEM (e4689), so Vera is most of the year's CPU revenue. The remainder is prior-generation CPUs in systems still shipping early in the year. No passage sizes that remainder. The rung holds for any share from about 84% up to 100% at the forecast pace.\",\n      \"rests_on\": [\"F6\", \"e4691\", \"e4856\", \"e4689\", \"e3660\", \"e4764\"],\n      \"outcome_for\": [],\n      \"reference_for\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F8\",\n      \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"scope\": \"Vera CPU\", \"period\": \"FY2028\"},\n      \"kind\": \"computed\",\n      \"basis\": \"forecast\",\n      \"value\": 32.4,\n      \"low\": null,\n      \"high\": null,\n      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n      \"evidence_id\": null,\n      \"quote_number\": null,\n      \"qualifier\": null,\n      \"inputs\": [\"F6\", \"F7\"],\n      \"operation\": \"share\",\n      \"basis_text\": null,\n      \"rests_on\": null,\n      \"outcome_for\": [\"v41\"],\n      \"reference_for\": []\n    }\n  ],\n  \"answers\": [\n    {\n      \"view_id\": \"v41\",\n      \"shape\": \"forecast\",\n      \"rung\": 3,\n      \"reference_unavailable\": null,\n      \"reasons\": \"Rung 3 (Neutral, $30-40 billion). I forecast about $32 billion of Vera CPU revenue in FY2028 (F8). Reference: NVIDIA's own outlook. CFO Colette Kress reaffirmed about $20 billion of CPU revenue in FY2027 and said FY2028 should more than double it (e3660). Huang and other outlets carry the same claim (e4764, e4691, e4856), counted once. That puts the reference above $40 billion (F3), at rung 2. No consensus figure is supplied. How I got the outcome: the FY2027 base is current-year guidance, so I take it in full (F1). The FY2028 doubling is a full-year target for the next fiscal year. All four selling quarters are still ahead, and it depends on a Vera ramp that the evidence does not yet show in volume: Anthropic is still evaluating Vera (e5044), and deployments are described in the future tense (e4689). So the target is discounted for time and execution rather than taken as the answer. Bounds on the growth pace: a vendor that only held share in a server-CPU market growing about 50% a year (AMD, e4687; BofA's fivefold growth from 2026 to 2030 is a similar pace, e4857) would grow about 1.5x, giving $30 billion. The guidance floor is 2.0x, giving $40 billion. Broad deployment claims (e4689) and Arm's 45% revenue share of data-center servers (e4692) support gaining share above the market pace. AMD's benchmark claims that its EPYC parts beat Vera by 1.2x per core up to 3.4x per modeled rack (e5399-e5404; a competitor's own tests, weighed lightly as one signal) argue against taking the guidance in full. I forecast 1.8x (F5), which gives $36 billion of total CPU revenue (F6). The question asks only about Vera. Management attributes the doubling to Vera (e4691, e4856), so I put Vera at 90% of FY2028 CPU revenue (F7). The rest is prior-generation CPUs in systems still shipping early in the year. That gives $32.4 billion. Sensitivity: rung 3 holds for any Vera share from about 84% to 100% at this pace. Reaching rung 2 needs the full guided 2x pace with nearly all CPU revenue being Vera. Falling to rung 4 needs a pace below about 1.67x at a 90% share. Scope note: two outlets describe the guidance as total CPU revenue (e3660, e4764) and two as Vera CPU revenue (e4691, e4856); the share figure covers that gap. Themes: our capex judgements put hyperscaler data-center spending at about $750 billion in 2026 (v6) and above $1.1 trillion in 2027 (v7, updated upward). That supports demand and keeps the pace well above the market's own growth, but on its own it does not supply the share gain that a pace above 2x needs.\",\n      \"why\": null,\n      \"cites\": [\"e3660\", \"e4691\", \"e4764\", \"e4856\", \"e4689\", \"e5044\", \"e4687\", \"e4857\", \"e4692\", \"e5399\", \"e5400\", \"e5401\", \"e5402\", \"e5403\", \"e5404\"],\n      \"pushes\": [\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e3660\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4691\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4764\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4856\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4689\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5044\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4692\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4687\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4857\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5399\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5400\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5401\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5402\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5403\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5404\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}\n      ],\n      \"theme_pushes\": [\n        {\"view_id\": \"v7\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n        {\"view_id\": \"v6\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"}\n      ]\n    }\n  ]\n}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json\n{\n  \"figures\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F1\",\n      \"what\": {\n        \"population\": \"NVIDIA\",\n        \"measure\": \"revenue\",\n        \"scope\": \"CPU\",\n        \"period\": \"FY2027\"\n      },\n      \"kind\": \"source\",\n      \"basis\": \"guidance\",\n      \"value\": 20,\n      \"low\": null,\n      \"high\": null,\n      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e3660\",\n      \"quote_number\": \"$20 billion\",\n      \"qualifier\": \"none\",\n      \"inputs\": null,\n      \"operation\": null,\n      \"basis_text\": null,\n      \"rests_on\": null,\n      \"outcome_for\": [],\n      \"reference_for\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F2\",\n      \"what\": {\n        \"population\": \"NVIDIA\",\n        \"measure\": \"revenue multiple\",\n        \"scope\": \"Vera CPU\",\n        \"period\": \"FY2028\"\n      },\n      \"kind\": \"source\",\n      \"basis\": \"guidance\",\n      \"value\": 2,\n      \"low\": null,\n      \"high\": null,\n      \"unit\": \"x\",\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e4691\",\n      \"quote_number\": \"double\",\n      \"qualifier\": \"none\",\n      \"inputs\": null,\n      \"operation\": null,\n      \"basis_text\": null,\n      \"rests_on\": null,\n      \"outcome_for\": [],\n      \"reference_for\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F3\",\n      \"what\": {\n        \"population\": \"NVIDIA\",\n        \"measure\": \"revenue\",\n        \"scope\": \"Vera CPU\",\n        \"period\": \"FY2028\"\n      },\n      \"kind\": \"computed\",\n      \"basis\": \"guidance\",\n      \"value\": 40,\n      \"low\": null,\n      \"high\": null,\n      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n      \"evidence_id\": null,\n      \"quote_number\": null,\n      \"qualifier\": null,\n      \"inputs\": [\"F1\", \"F2\"],\n      \"operation\": \"product\",\n      \"basis_text\": null,\n      \"rests_on\": null,\n      \"outcome_for\": [],\n      \"reference_for\": [\"v41\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F4\",\n      \"what\": {\n        \"population\": \"NVIDIA\",\n        \"measure\": \"share of target revenue realized\",\n        \"scope\": \"Vera CPU\",\n        \"period\": \"FY2028\"\n      },\n      \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n      \"basis\": \"forecast\",\n      \"value\": 90,\n      \"low\": null,\n      \"high\": null,\n      \"unit\": \"%\",\n      \"evidence_id\": null,\n      \"quote_number\": null,\n      \"qualifier\": null,\n      \"inputs\": null,\n      \"operation\": null,\n      \"basis_text\": \"I estimate that NVIDIA realizes 90% of the reference. The announced breadth of deployments supports most of the ramp. Anthropic is still evaluating Vera, and AMD's agentic-workload tests show a competitive alternative. I allow for some planned sales to slip or go to a rival, equivalent to less than half an average quarter of revenue at the target pace. The passages give no order sizes or shipment pace to narrow that estimate.\",\n      \"rests_on\": [\"F3\", \"e4689\", \"e5044\", \"e5403\"],\n      \"outcome_for\": [],\n      \"reference_for\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F5\",\n      \"what\": {\n        \"population\": \"NVIDIA\",\n        \"measure\": \"revenue\",\n        \"scope\": \"Vera CPU\",\n        \"period\": \"FY2028\"\n      },\n      \"kind\": \"computed\",\n      \"basis\": \"forecast\",\n      \"value\": 36,\n      \"low\": null,\n      \"high\": null,\n      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n      \"evidence_id\": null,\n      \"quote_number\": null,\n      \"qualifier\": null,\n      \"inputs\": [\"F3\", \"F4\"],\n      \"operation\": \"share\",\n      \"basis_text\": null,\n      \"rests_on\": null,\n      \"outcome_for\": [\"v41\"],\n      \"reference_for\": []\n    }\n  ],\n  \"answers\": [\n    {\n      \"view_id\": \"v41\",\n      \"shape\": \"forecast\",\n      \"rung\": 3,\n      \"reference_unavailable\": null,\n      \"reasons\": \"Management's doubling outlook gives a $40 billion reference at the lower edge of rung 2. The Vera-specific reporting ties that outlook to the $20 billion FY2027 CPU baseline. Separate reports of potential to more than double describe the same outlook and leave the upside unsized. NVIDIA expects broad deployment, and rising hyperscaler spending supports demand. Anthropic is still evaluating Vera, while AMD's own benchmarks show a credible alternative for agentic workloads. Those benchmarks indicate competitive risk without quantifying lost sales. I expect most of the planned ramp to land, with a modest shortfall from deployment timing and customer choice. Realizing 90% of the reference puts my FY2028 forecast at $36 billion, within rung 3.\",\n      \"why\": null,\n      \"cites\": [\"e3660\", \"e4691\", \"e4856\", \"e4689\", \"e5044\", \"e5403\"],\n      \"pushes\": [\n        {\n          \"evidence_id\": \"e3660\",\n          \"push\": \"none\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"evidence_id\": \"e4691\",\n          \"push\": \"none\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"evidence_id\": \"e4856\",\n          \"push\": \"none\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"evidence_id\": \"e4689\",\n          \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"evidence_id\": \"e5044\",\n          \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"evidence_id\": \"e5403\",\n          \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"theme_pushes\": [\n        {\n          \"view_id\": \"v6\",\n          \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"view_id\": \"v7\",\n          \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n        }\n      ]\n    }\n  ]\n}poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json\n{\"figures\":[{\"id\":\"F1\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue\",\"scope\":\"CPU\",\"period\":\"FY2027\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"guidance\",\"value\":20,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":\"e3660\",\"quote_number\":\"$20 billion\",\"qualifier\":\"none\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F2\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue multiple\",\"scope\":\"CPU\",\"period\":\"FY2028\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"guidance\",\"value\":2,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"x\",\"evidence_id\":\"e3660\",\"quote_number\":\"double\",\"qualifier\":\"more than\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F3\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue\",\"scope\":\"CPU\",\"period\":\"FY2028\"},\"kind\":\"computed\",\"basis\":\"guidance\",\"value\":40,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":null,\"quote_number\":null,\"qualifier\":null,\"inputs\":[\"F1\",\"F2\"],\"operation\":\"product\",\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[\"v41\"]},{\"id\":\"F4\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue multiple\",\"scope\":\"CPU\",\"period\":\"FY2028\"},\"kind\":\"estimate\",\"basis\":\"forecast\",\"value\":2.1,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"x\",\"evidence_id\":null,\"quote_number\":null,\"qualifier\":null,\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":\"My forecast pace for FY2028 CPU revenue over FY2027. The benchmark is NVIDIA's guidance that FY2028 CPU revenue will more than double (F2, repeated by the CEO), which sets a floor of 2.0x. The base is reaffirmed for the current year, so the line is already selling; stated deployment at every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab and system OEM and Anthropic's evaluation of Vera for agentic work support a small overshoot. AMD's benchmark claims against Vera trim the upside, so the pace stays well short of the 2.5x that $50 billion would need.\",\"rests_on\":[\"F2\",\"e3660\",\"e4764\",\"e4689\",\"e5044\",\"e5400\",\"e5401\",\"e5402\"],\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F5\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue\",\"scope\":\"CPU\",\"period\":\"FY2028\"},\"kind\":\"computed\",\"basis\":\"forecast\",\"value\":42,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":null,\"quote_number\":null,\"qualifier\":null,\"inputs\":[\"F1\",\"F4\"],\"operation\":\"product\",\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F6\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue share\",\"scope\":\"Vera CPU within CPU\",\"period\":\"FY2028\"},\"kind\":\"estimate\",\"basis\":\"forecast\",\"value\":100,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"%\",\"evidence_id\":null,\"quote_number\":null,\"qualifier\":null,\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":\"NVIDIA describes the FY2028 doubling of its CPU revenue as Vera CPU revenue, doubling the $20 billion it cited for FY2027 (e4691, e4856). No passage sizes any other NVIDIA CPU line for FY2028, so I take Vera as the whole of FY2028 CPU revenue.\",\"rests_on\":[\"e4691\",\"e4856\",\"F5\"],\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F7\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue\",\"scope\":\"Vera CPU\",\"period\":\"FY2028\"},\"kind\":\"computed\",\"basis\":\"forecast\",\"value\":42,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":null,\"quote_number\":null,\"qualifier\":null,\"inputs\":[\"F5\",\"F6\"],\"operation\":\"share\",\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[\"v41\"],\"reference_for\":[]}],\"answers\":[{\"view_id\":\"v41\",\"shape\":\"forecast\",\"rung\":2,\"reference_unavailable\":null,\"reasons\":\"Reference: NVIDIA's guidance. The CFO said NVIDIA still expects $20 billion of CPU revenue in FY2027 and expects FY2028 to more than double it (e3660); the CEO and management repeat the same claim (e4764, e4856), and e4691 and e4856 state it as Vera CPU revenue. Two times $20 billion is $40 billion (F3), and 'more than double' puts the reference just inside rung 2 ($40 billion to under $50 billion). Which band the evidence favours: rung 2. The FY2027 base is reaffirmed for the current year, so the CPU line is already selling at volume rather than a product with no shipments; the FY2028 growth rests on stated deployment by every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab and system OEM (e4689) and new demand for CPU-heavy agentic work (Anthropic is evaluating Vera, e5044). Against it, AMD's benchmark claims (1.2x per-core and 2.24x platform performance, e5400; 3.4x throughput in a modeled rack, e5401; 37% and 2.37x suite gains, e5402) show a competitor attacking Vera, but they are AMD's own performance measurements and name no lost customer or volume, so they trim the upside rather than cut the year below the guided doubling. I forecast a pace of 2.1x (my estimate, F4): the guided floor of 2.0x plus a small overshoot, well short of the 2.5x that $50 billion would need. 2.1 x $20 billion = $42 billion of CPU revenue (F5). NVIDIA describes the FY2028 figure as Vera CPU revenue and no passage sizes another CPU line for FY2028, so Vera is taken as all of it (F6), giving $42 billion (F7), rung 2. The band holds for any pace from 2.0x to just under 2.5x; it would fall to rung 3 only if NVIDIA missed its own doubling or another CPU line took part of FY2028 CPU revenue, and the evidence shows neither. The CPU market forecasts (Bank of America: $35 billion in 2026 to $170 billion in 2030, e4857; AMD: 50% annual growth to a $220 billion opportunity, e4687) describe a fast-growing market but do not reconcile with NVIDIA's own figure (NVIDIA's $20 billion would already be 57% of Bank of America's 2026 market), so they do not size NVIDIA's revenue and move nothing; the Arm server share (e4692) is a past share with no NVIDIA figure. Themes: the 2027 hyperscaler capex theme (v7, updated, above the $1.1 trillion boundary and above the market's reference) and the 2026 capex theme (v6, upper-middle) support the spending behind the Vera platform and push toward higher rungs.\",\"why\":null,\"cites\":[\"e3660\",\"e4691\",\"e4764\",\"e4856\",\"e4689\",\"e5044\",\"e5400\",\"e5401\",\"e5402\",\"e4857\",\"e4687\"],\"pushes\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e3660\",\"push\":\"toward_first\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4691\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4764\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4856\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4689\",\"push\":\"toward_first\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5044\",\"push\":\"toward_first\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4687\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4857\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4692\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5399\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5400\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5401\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5402\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5403\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5404\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"}],\"theme_pushes\":[{\"view_id\":\"v7\",\"push\":\"toward_first\"},{\"view_id\":\"v6\",\"push\":\"toward_first\"}]}]}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json\n{\n  \"figures\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F1\",\n      \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"scope\": \"CPU\", \"period\": \"FY2027\"},\n      \"kind\": \"source\",\n      \"basis\": \"guidance\",\n      \"value\": 20,\n      \"low\": null,\n      \"high\": null,\n      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e3660\",\n      \"quote_number\": \"$20 billion\",\n      \"qualifier\": \"none\",\n      \"inputs\": null,\n      \"operation\": null,\n      \"basis_text\": null,\n      \"rests_on\": null,\n      \"outcome_for\": [],\n      \"reference_for\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F2\",\n      \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue multiple\", \"scope\": \"CPU\", \"period\": \"FY2028 relative to FY2027\"},\n      \"kind\": \"source\",\n      \"basis\": \"guidance\",\n      \"value\": 2,\n      \"low\": null,\n      \"high\": null,\n      \"unit\": \"x\",\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e3660\",\n      \"quote_number\": \"double\",\n      \"qualifier\": \"more than\",\n      \"inputs\": null,\n      \"operation\": null,\n      \"basis_text\": null,\n      \"rests_on\": null,\n      \"outcome_for\": [],\n      \"reference_for\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F3\",\n      \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"scope\": \"CPU, threshold management expects to exceed\", \"period\": \"FY2028\"},\n      \"kind\": \"computed\",\n      \"basis\": \"guidance\",\n      \"value\": 40,\n      \"low\": null,\n      \"high\": null,\n      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n      \"evidence_id\": null,\n      \"quote_number\": null,\n      \"qualifier\": null,\n      \"inputs\": [\"F1\", \"F2\"],\n      \"operation\": \"product\",\n      \"basis_text\": null,\n      \"rests_on\": null,\n      \"outcome_for\": [],\n      \"reference_for\": []\n    }\n  ],\n  \"answers\": [\n    {\n      \"view_id\": \"v41\",\n      \"shape\": \"unresolved\",\n      \"rung\": null,\n      \"reference_unavailable\": null,\n      \"reasons\": \"Management's Vera-specific outlook points to $40 billion, the lower edge of rung 2. The CFO expects CPU revenue to more than double from a projected $20 billion base. The Vera-specific accounts describe the same outlook as doubling or potentially more than doubling. They give no amount above $40 billion. NVIDIA expects broad deployment, and Anthropic's evaluation identifies a customer and a use for Vera. Strong hyperscaler spending, particularly in 2027, supports demand during the selling period. AMD's agentic-workload tests identify a reason that same customer might choose EPYC. Neither the evaluation nor the tests disclose a purchasing decision. Industry growth forecasts and Arm's server revenue share cannot tell us how many Vera CPUs customers will buy.\",\n      \"why\": \"Both the FY2027 base and the FY2028 increase are projections. No passage gives a Vera shipment run rate, an order with units and pricing, or a customer deployment schedule from which to estimate FY2028 sales. A modest shortfall from doubling the projected base could put Vera below $40 billion, while delivery on the plan could put it above. The evidence does not favor a particular revenue band strongly enough to size that difference.\",\n      \"cites\": [\"e3660\", \"e4691\", \"e4856\", \"e4689\", \"e5044\", \"e5403\", \"e4687\", \"e4857\", \"e4692\"],\n      \"pushes\": [\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e3660\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4687\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4689\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4691\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4692\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4764\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4856\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4857\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5044\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5399\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5400\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5401\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5402\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5403\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5404\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}\n      ],\n      \"theme_pushes\": [\n        {\"view_id\": \"v6\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n        {\"view_id\": \"v7\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"}\n      ]\n    }\n  ]\n}poc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json\n{\"figures\":[{\"id\":\"F1\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue\",\"scope\":\"CPU\",\"period\":\"FY2027\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"guidance\",\"value\":20,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":\"e3660\",\"quote_number\":\"$20 billion\",\"qualifier\":\"none\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F2\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue growth multiple\",\"scope\":\"CPU\",\"period\":\"FY2028\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"guidance\",\"value\":2,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"x\",\"evidence_id\":\"e3660\",\"quote_number\":\"double\",\"qualifier\":\"more than\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F3\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue\",\"scope\":\"Vera CPU\",\"period\":\"FY2028\"},\"kind\":\"computed\",\"basis\":\"guidance\",\"value\":40,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":null,\"quote_number\":null,\"qualifier\":null,\"inputs\":[\"F1\",\"F2\"],\"operation\":\"product\",\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[\"v41\"]},{\"id\":\"F4\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"CPU market\",\"measure\":\"revenue\",\"scope\":\"all vendors\",\"period\":\"CY2026\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"third_party\",\"value\":35,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":\"e4857\",\"quote_number\":\"$35 billion\",\"qualifier\":\"none\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F5\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"CPU market\",\"measure\":\"revenue\",\"scope\":\"all vendors\",\"period\":\"CY2030\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"third_party\",\"value\":170,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":\"e4857\",\"quote_number\":\"$170 billion\",\"qualifier\":\"none\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F6\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"CPU market\",\"measure\":\"revenue growth multiple\",\"scope\":\"all vendors\",\"period\":\"CY2026 to CY2030\"},\"kind\":\"computed\",\"basis\":\"third_party\",\"value\":4.857,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"x\",\"evidence_id\":null,\"quote_number\":null,\"qualifier\":null,\"inputs\":[\"F5\",\"F4\"],\"operation\":\"ratio\",\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F7\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"server CPU market\",\"measure\":\"revenue growth rate\",\"scope\":\"annual\",\"period\":\"CY2026 to CY2030\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"third_party\",\"value\":50,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"%\",\"evidence_id\":\"e4687\",\"quote_number\":\"50%\",\"qualifier\":\"none\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F8\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue growth multiple\",\"scope\":\"Vera CPU\",\"period\":\"FY2028\"},\"kind\":\"estimate\",\"basis\":\"forecast\",\"value\":1.75,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"x\",\"evidence_id\":null,\"quote_number\":null,\"qualifier\":null,\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":\"My estimate of the FY2028 growth pace over the FY2027 base. The floor is the market's pace: Bank of America's path of 4.857x over 2026-2030 (F6) works out to about 1.48x a year, and AMD's server CPU market growth of 50% a year (F7) is 1.5x; holding share means growing about 1.5x. The ceiling is management's 'more than double' (F2), which needs NVIDIA to gain share in a single year. Deployment breadth (every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab and OEM, e4689) and Anthropic's evaluation (e5044) support at least holding share and some gain, but no order, customer volume or capacity figure puts the extra volume in hand, Anthropic is still evaluating rather than buying, and AMD claims materially better per-core, platform and rack performance (e5399, e5400, e5401, e5402, e5404; a competitor's own benchmarks, weak but the only competitive evidence). I put the pace at the midpoint between market pace and management's target, 1.75x.\",\"rests_on\":[\"F2\",\"F6\",\"F7\",\"e4689\",\"e5044\",\"e5399\",\"e5400\",\"e5401\",\"e5402\",\"e5404\"],\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F9\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue\",\"scope\":\"Vera CPU\",\"period\":\"FY2028\"},\"kind\":\"computed\",\"basis\":\"forecast\",\"value\":35,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":null,\"quote_number\":null,\"qualifier\":null,\"inputs\":[\"F1\",\"F8\"],\"operation\":\"product\",\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[\"v41\"],\"reference_for\":[]}],\"answers\":[{\"view_id\":\"v41\",\"shape\":\"forecast\",\"rung\":3,\"reference_unavailable\":null,\"reasons\":\"Reference: company guidance. The CFO still expects $20 billion of CPU revenue in FY2027 and expects FY2028 to more than double it (e3660; the same claim is repeated by the CEO and other outlets, e4691, e4764, e4856, and counts once). That puts the reference at $40 billion or more (F3), at the lower edge of rung 2 (at least $40 billion, below $50 billion). The company itself applies this doubling to Vera CPU revenue (e4691, e4856), so I treat the FY2027 base as the base for Vera; no evidence sizes any other CPU within it.\\n\\nWhich band the evidence favours: rung 3. FY2028 is a full fiscal year that starts in February 2027. The doubling is a management target for a product whose broad deployment is still described in the future tense (e4689), and a named customer is only evaluating it (e5044). No order, customer demand number or supply figure puts more than $40 billion of volume in hand for the year. Under the evidence requirement, the target is weighed as an input and not taken in full. Two outside market paths set the pace NVIDIA gets by holding share. Bank of America sees the CPU market going from $35 billion in 2026 to $170 billion in 2030 (e4857), 4.857x (F6), about 1.48x a year. AMD sees server CPU revenue growing 50% a year (e4687), which is 1.5x. Growing at market pace from the $20 billion base gives about $30 billion. Doubling needs share gains in one year, while AMD claims 1.2x Vera's per-core performance, 2.24x its platform performance and 3.4x its rack throughput (e5400, e5401), plus further suite and memory-bandwidth leads (e5399, e5402, e5404). These are a competitor's own benchmarks and weigh lightly, but nothing in the evidence counters them. On the other side, Vera is to be deployed by every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab and OEM (e4689), and Arm-based servers already took 45% of data-center revenue share (e4692). Both support holding share and gaining some.\\n\\nSizing: I estimate a 1.75x pace (F8), the midpoint between market pace and management's target. That gives $20 billion x 1.75 = $35 billion (F9), inside rung 3 ($30 billion to below $40 billion). The band holds across the plausible range. Any pace from 1.5x up to just under 2.0x gives $30 billion to $39.9 billion, all rung 3. Rung 2 needs the management target taken in full, which the evidence does not yet support with volume. Rung 4 needs NVIDIA to grow more slowly than the market, and no delay, cancellation or cut in the evidence points there. Theme v7 (hyperscaler data-center capex in calendar 2027 judged just above $1.1 trillion, updated) supports strong buyer spending through most of FY2028. It backs at least market-pace growth and pushes toward the first rung, but it does not size Vera volume, so the outcome stays in rung 3.\",\"why\":null,\"cites\":[\"e3660\",\"e4691\",\"e4687\",\"e4857\",\"e4689\",\"e4692\",\"e5044\",\"e5399\",\"e5400\",\"e5401\",\"e5402\",\"e5404\"],\"pushes\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e3660\",\"push\":\"toward_first\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4691\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4764\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4856\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4687\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4857\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4689\",\"push\":\"toward_first\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4692\",\"push\":\"toward_first\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5044\",\"push\":\"toward_first\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5399\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5400\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5401\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5402\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5403\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5404\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"}],\"theme_pushes\":[{\"view_id\":\"v7\",\"push\":\"toward_first\"}]}]}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json\n{\n  \"figures\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F1\",\n      \"what\": {\n        \"population\": \"NVIDIA\",\n        \"measure\": \"revenue\",\n        \"scope\": \"CPU\",\n        \"period\": \"FY2027\"\n      },\n      \"kind\": \"source\",\n      \"basis\": \"guidance\",\n      \"value\": 20,\n      \"low\": null,\n      \"high\": null,\n      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e3660\",\n      \"quote_number\": \"$20 billion\",\n      \"qualifier\": \"none\",\n      \"inputs\": null,\n      \"operation\": null,\n      \"basis_text\": null,\n      \"rests_on\": null,\n      \"outcome_for\": [],\n      \"reference_for\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F2\",\n      \"what\": {\n        \"population\": \"NVIDIA\",\n        \"measure\": \"year-over-year revenue multiple\",\n        \"scope\": \"Vera CPU\",\n        \"period\": \"FY2028\"\n      },\n      \"kind\": \"source\",\n      \"basis\": \"guidance\",\n      \"value\": 2,\n      \"low\": null,\n      \"high\": null,\n      \"unit\": \"x\",\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e4691\",\n      \"quote_number\": \"double\",\n      \"qualifier\": \"none\",\n      \"inputs\": null,\n      \"operation\": null,\n      \"basis_text\": null,\n      \"rests_on\": null,\n      \"outcome_for\": [],\n      \"reference_for\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F3\",\n      \"what\": {\n        \"population\": \"NVIDIA\",\n        \"measure\": \"revenue\",\n        \"scope\": \"Vera CPU\",\n        \"period\": \"FY2028\"\n      },\n      \"kind\": \"computed\",\n      \"basis\": \"guidance\",\n      \"value\": 40,\n      \"low\": null,\n      \"high\": null,\n      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n      \"evidence_id\": null,\n      \"quote_number\": null,\n      \"qualifier\": null,\n      \"inputs\": [\"F1\", \"F2\"],\n      \"operation\": \"product\",\n      \"basis_text\": null,\n      \"rests_on\": null,\n      \"outcome_for\": [],\n      \"reference_for\": []\n    }\n  ],\n  \"answers\": [\n    {\n      \"view_id\": \"v41\",\n      \"shape\": \"unresolved\",\n      \"rung\": null,\n      \"reference_unavailable\": null,\n      \"reasons\": \"Management's reference starts at $40 billion, the lower edge of rung 2. The CFO described $20 billion as expected FY2027 CPU revenue. The Vera-specific accounts identify that base with Vera and describe FY2028 revenue as doubling, with potential for more. These accounts repeat one outlook. NVIDIA expects broad deployment, and Anthropic's evaluation identifies a customer and a use for Vera. The updated 2027 hyperscaler spending outlook strengthens the demand case during NVIDIA's FY2028 selling period, but customers can spend those budgets on GPUs, infrastructure or competing CPUs. AMD's benchmark results give buyers a concrete reason to consider EPYC. They disclose no order that switched from Vera or price concession that NVIDIA made.\",\n      \"why\": \"The FY2027 base is itself a target. No passage gives Vera shipments against that base, FY2028 delivery schedules, committed units or realized prices. Broad customer interest supports a ramp, but its scale is unknown. There is no basis for deciding whether enough purchases will close in FY2028 to reach $40 billion, or for sizing a shortfall into a particular lower band.\",\n      \"cites\": [\"e3660\", \"e4691\", \"e4856\", \"e4689\", \"e5044\", \"e5400\", \"e5401\", \"e5403\"],\n      \"pushes\": [\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e3660\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4687\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4689\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4691\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4692\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4764\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4856\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4857\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5044\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5399\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5400\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5401\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5402\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5403\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5404\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}\n      ],\n      \"theme_pushes\": [\n        {\"view_id\": \"v6\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n        {\"view_id\": \"v7\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"}\n      ]\n    }\n  ]\n}","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -Bc 'import json,glob\nfor p in sorted(glob.glob(\\\"poc/data/judging-v13s-blind[56]-*/S/answers/*.json\\\")+glob.glob(\\\"poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-*/E/answers/*.json\\\")):\n if \\\"blind5\\\" in p and \\\"claude\\\" in p: continue\n d=json.load(open(p));print(p)\n for f in d[\\\"figures\\\"]:\n  if f[\\\"basis_text\\\"]:print(f[\\\"id\\\"],f[\\\"basis_text\\\"])\n for a in d[\\\"answers\\\"]:print(a[\\\"reasons\\\"],a[\\\"why\\\"])'\nfor f in poc/data/judging-v13s-blind{3,4,5,6}-*/{S,E}/answers/*.json; do echo \\\"\"'$f $(rg -n '\"'\\\"reasons\\\":|\\\"why\\\": \\\"' \\\"\"'$f\" | cut -d: -f1 | xargs)\"; done'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -Bc 'import json,glob\nfor p in sorted(glob.glob(\\\"poc/data/judging-v13s-blind[56]-*/S/answers/*.json\\\")+glob.glob(\\\"poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-*/E/answers/*.json\\\")):\n if \\\"blind5\\\" in p and \\\"claude\\\" in p: continue\n d=json.load(open(p));print(p)\n for f in d[\\\"figures\\\"]:\n  if f[\\\"basis_text\\\"]:print(f[\\\"id\\\"],f[\\\"basis_text\\\"])\n for a in d[\\\"answers\\\"]:print(a[\\\"reasons\\\"],a[\\\"why\\\"])'\nfor f in poc/data/judging-v13s-blind{3,4,5,6}-*/{S,E}/answers/*.json; do echo \\\"\"'$f $(rg -n '\"'\\\"reasons\\\":|\\\"why\\\": \\\"' \\\"\"'$f\" | cut -d: -f1 | xargs)\"; done'","aggregated_output":"poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json\nF5 My forecast of FY2028 CPU revenue growth over FY2027 is 1.8x. Management's outlook of more than 2x (F2) is a full-year target for the next fiscal year: all four selling quarters are still ahead, and it rests on a Vera ramp that no passage yet shows in volume. So it is discounted for time and execution. Anthropic is only evaluating Vera (e5044), deployments are described in the future tense (e4689), and AMD claims its EPYC parts beat Vera by 1.2x per core up to 3.4x per modeled rack (e5399-e5404, a competitor's own tests, weighed lightly as one signal). The lower anchor is the outside market pace of about 50% a year (F4; BofA's fivefold growth from 2026 to 2030 in e4857 is a similar pace), which is what a vendor that only holds its share would reach (1.5x). NVIDIA's claim of deployment at every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab and OEM (e4689) and Arm's 45% revenue share of data-center servers (e4692) support gaining share above that pace. 1.8x sits between the market pace (1.5x) and the guidance floor (2.0x), weighted toward the guidance because the CFO reaffirmed it.\nF7 I estimate Vera at 90% of NVIDIA's FY2028 CPU revenue. Management attributes the FY2028 doubling to Vera itself (e4691: 'Nvidia expects its Vera CPU revenue to double next year'; e4856), and Vera is to be deployed by every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab and system OEM (e4689), so Vera is most of the year's CPU revenue. The remainder is prior-generation CPUs in systems still shipping early in the year. No passage sizes that remainder. The rung holds for any share from about 84% up to 100% at the forecast pace.\nRung 3 (Neutral, $30-40 billion). I forecast about $32 billion of Vera CPU revenue in FY2028 (F8). Reference: NVIDIA's own outlook. CFO Colette Kress reaffirmed about $20 billion of CPU revenue in FY2027 and said FY2028 should more than double it (e3660). Huang and other outlets carry the same claim (e4764, e4691, e4856), counted once. That puts the reference above $40 billion (F3), at rung 2. No consensus figure is supplied. How I got the outcome: the FY2027 base is current-year guidance, so I take it in full (F1). The FY2028 doubling is a full-year target for the next fiscal year. All four selling quarters are still ahead, and it depends on a Vera ramp that the evidence does not yet show in volume: Anthropic is still evaluating Vera (e5044), and deployments are described in the future tense (e4689). So the target is discounted for time and execution rather than taken as the answer. Bounds on the growth pace: a vendor that only held share in a server-CPU market growing about 50% a year (AMD, e4687; BofA's fivefold growth from 2026 to 2030 is a similar pace, e4857) would grow about 1.5x, giving $30 billion. The guidance floor is 2.0x, giving $40 billion. Broad deployment claims (e4689) and Arm's 45% revenue share of data-center servers (e4692) support gaining share above the market pace. AMD's benchmark claims that its EPYC parts beat Vera by 1.2x per core up to 3.4x per modeled rack (e5399-e5404; a competitor's own tests, weighed lightly as one signal) argue against taking the guidance in full. I forecast 1.8x (F5), which gives $36 billion of total CPU revenue (F6). The question asks only about Vera. Management attributes the doubling to Vera (e4691, e4856), so I put Vera at 90% of FY2028 CPU revenue (F7). The rest is prior-generation CPUs in systems still shipping early in the year. That gives $32.4 billion. Sensitivity: rung 3 holds for any Vera share from about 84% to 100% at this pace. Reaching rung 2 needs the full guided 2x pace with nearly all CPU revenue being Vera. Falling to rung 4 needs a pace below about 1.67x at a 90% share. Scope note: two outlets describe the guidance as total CPU revenue (e3660, e4764) and two as Vera CPU revenue (e4691, e4856); the share figure covers that gap. Themes: our capex judgements put hyperscaler data-center spending at about $750 billion in 2026 (v6) and above $1.1 trillion in 2027 (v7, updated upward). That supports demand and keeps the pace well above the market's own growth, but on its own it does not supply the share gain that a pace above 2x needs. None\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json\nF4 I estimate that NVIDIA realizes 90% of the reference. The announced breadth of deployments supports most of the ramp. Anthropic is still evaluating Vera, and AMD's agentic-workload tests show a competitive alternative. I allow for some planned sales to slip or go to a rival, equivalent to less than half an average quarter of revenue at the target pace. The passages give no order sizes or shipment pace to narrow that estimate.\nManagement's doubling outlook gives a $40 billion reference at the lower edge of rung 2. The Vera-specific reporting ties that outlook to the $20 billion FY2027 CPU baseline. Separate reports of potential to more than double describe the same outlook and leave the upside unsized. NVIDIA expects broad deployment, and rising hyperscaler spending supports demand. Anthropic is still evaluating Vera, while AMD's own benchmarks show a credible alternative for agentic workloads. Those benchmarks indicate competitive risk without quantifying lost sales. I expect most of the planned ramp to land, with a modest shortfall from deployment timing and customer choice. Realizing 90% of the reference puts my FY2028 forecast at $36 billion, within rung 3. None\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/S/answers/codex.json\nF5 I favor the $700 billion to below $800 billion band. The four companies' AI infrastructure plan covers the right buyers and year. Amazon's memory purchases and the capacity needed to serve Azure's backlog point to servers and the facilities that house them as the main use of that money. For data-center capex to fall below $700 billion, spending outside data centers and spending deferred beyond year-end would together have to absorb more than $30 billion of the indicated plan, even before allowing for conventional cloud equipment bought for the same facilities. The project-delay tally does not identify that much spending due from these buyers this year. I use $730 billion as a rounded estimate near the stated floor of the AI plan. The four-company total capex estimate limits the likely upside, and the broader data-center estimates support the scale without supplying a precise split.\nI forecast about $730 billion. The four companies' stated AI infrastructure plan has its lower edge in rung 4. Amazon raised its budget as memory became more expensive, so buying the same hardware can now require more dollars. Microsoft's Azure backlog gives it a reason to keep buying capacity. U.S. construction delays weigh against full execution, although the tally measures whole projects across owners and years. On the upside, $800 billion would already exceed the outside estimate of these four companies' entire capital budgets. NVIDIA approaches that figure only by including a fifth buyer. None\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23/S/answers/claude.json\nF12 No passage states Microsoft's calendar-2026 capital spending. The four-company tally less the three calendar-year guides leaves $150-205bn (F6). Microsoft's Jan-Mar 2026 quarter of $30.88bn, up 84% a year (about 16% a quarter), held at that pace reaches only about the low end of that span on the quoted figure; the $678bn Azure backlog supports a continued ramp, and the other four-company tallies (near $745bn, $760bn) imply a figure in the middle or upper part of the span. I take $175bn, just below the span's midpoint to give weight to the reported run-rate.\nF21 The share of the four companies' capital spending that goes to data centers (servers, memory, network, buildings) rather than other assets such as Amazon's logistics network or offices. No passage sizes the non-data-center part. Meta's AI spending alone ($130bn) is 90-104% of its guide, about 96% at the midpoint, and data-center spending also covers non-AI compute; Amazon's 2026 raise was mostly memory chips, i.e. servers; the companies' indicated AI-infrastructure spending is more than $730bn against total-capex tallies of $720-760bn; and outside parties treat total capex as data-center spending ($750bn estimate; NVIDIA's top-five total capex of nearly $800bn, with BofA's five-company total at $795bn, relabelled as data-center capex). I deduct 3% for the unsized non-data-center items. A deduction large enough to put the outcome under $700bn would have to exceed about 4% of the total at its midpoint (6% at its top), more than the non-AI remainder of Meta's guide.\nReference: outside tallies put the four companies' guided 2026 capital spending at roughly $720-745bn (Zacks, e5014; the same range in e2079). Read as data-center spending, as outside parties do, it sits on rung 4; after my 3% data-center deduction it would straddle rungs 3 and 4 ($698-723bn). Build: Alphabet guides $195-205bn (raised from $180-190bn, e1254), Meta $125-145bn (raised, e1549) and Amazon $220bn (raised from $200bn, mostly on memory costs, e1649), together $540-570bn. No passage states Microsoft's calendar-2026 total; the tally less those guides leaves $150-205bn. Microsoft's Jan-Mar 2026 quarter ($30.88bn, up 84%, e922) held at its trailing pace reaches only about the low end of that span, while the $678bn Azure backlog (e4405) and the other tallies (near $745bn, e1856; $760bn, e2458) point higher, so I take $175bn (my estimate). Total capital expenditure: $715-745bn. The data-center share decides between rungs 3 and 4, and the evidence favours a small deduction and so rung 4: Meta's AI spending alone is $130bn (e3686), 90-104% of its guide; Amazon's raise went mostly to memory chips, i.e. servers (e1649); the companies' indicated AI-infrastructure spending is more than $730bn (e1947); and outside parties treat total capex as data-center spending ($750bn estimate, e1416; NVIDIA's top-five total capex of nearly $800bn, e2759, relabelled as data-center capex in e4766; BofA's five-company total $795bn, e5055). Non-data-center items (Amazon's logistics network, offices) are sized nowhere, so I deduct 3% (my estimate). Outcome: about $708bn (range $694-723bn), inside rung 4 ($700-800bn). Falling under $700bn would need non-data-center spending above about 4% of the total at its midpoint (6% at its top), more than the non-AI remainder of Meta's guide; and with every calendar-year guide raised this year and memory costs still rising (e4567), the total's upper half is likelier than its lower half, so the margin over $700bn, though thin, is more likely to widen than close. Against: the $130bn of U.S. data-center projects blocked or delayed so far in 2026 (e122) is a tally across all developers, not sized for these four or shown to cut their guided totals; Moody's and the BIS financing warnings (e2081, e4807) carry no number. Missing confirmation of a cut is not a cut. Rung 5 would need total capex above about $825bn, beyond every tally. NVIDIA's many repeats of its top-five figure count as one claim, and e2079/e1947 are another outlet of the same guided total as e5014. None\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23/S/answers/codex.json\nF4 I favor rung 4. Against the four companies’ own AI infrastructure spending indication, falling below $700 billion would require more than $30 billion of that plan to fall outside CY2026 data-center capex, even before counting conventional cloud investment. Amazon tied its higher budget chiefly to memory costs, and the same four companies have signed data-center leases. Both point to spending on the equipment and facilities the question covers. I estimate that 93–99% of tracked total capex reaches data centers. The upper end allows for conventional cloud facilities as well as AI. The lower end allows for other corporate investment, differences in what the companies call AI infrastructure, and construction delays. The passages do not disclose the actual split.\nI expect about $715 billion, in rung 4. The four-company total is tracking near $745 billion. At that level, just under 94% must go to data centers to reach $700 billion. The named companies’ AI infrastructure plans, Amazon’s higher memory bill and demand already booked by Azure make clearing that threshold more likely than missing it. My range extends into rung 3 because some planned facilities could slip. The project-delay tally covers the whole US and gives no CY2026 spending loss for these four, so subtracting its face value would overstate the effect. Likewise, signed leases indicate demand, but their full multiyear value does not enter this year’s capex. Rung 5 would require data-center spending alone to exceed the four companies’ tracked total. None\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/answers/claude.json 276\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/answers/codex.json 257\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/answers/claude.json 162\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/answers/codex.json 178\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/answers/claude.json 390\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/answers/codex.json 257\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json 162\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json 130\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/S/answers/claude.json 1\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/S/answers/codex.json 105\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json 1\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json 67 68\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23/S/answers/claude.json 1\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23/S/answers/codex.json 44\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json 1\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json 82 83\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'd=docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers\nnl -ba \"$d/BLIND6-RESULT.md\" | head -n 90\nnl -ba \"$d/BLIND5-RESULT.md\" | head -n 50\nrg -n '\"'T15|U06|1.85' \\\"\"'$d\"/{BLIND3-RESULT,BLIND4-RESULT,REREAD*}.md\nnl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-*/E/system.txt | head -n 30\nrg -n '\"'730 billion|memory chips|backlog|range of .720|720 billion to|blocked or delayed' poc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-*/S/user.txt | head -n 22\nrg -n 'D750|D749|68' docs/CURRENT_STATE.md | head -n 8\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'd=docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers\nnl -ba \"$d/BLIND6-RESULT.md\" | head -n 90\nnl -ba \"$d/BLIND5-RESULT.md\" | head -n 50\nrg -n '\"'T15|U06|1.85' \\\"\"'$d\"/{BLIND3-RESULT,BLIND4-RESULT,REREAD*}.md\nnl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-*/E/system.txt | head -n 30\nrg -n '\"'730 billion|memory chips|backlog|range of .720|720 billion to|blocked or delayed' poc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-*/S/user.txt | head -n 22\nrg -n 'D750|D749|68' docs/CURRENT_STATE.md | head -n 8\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# The D750 second writer test: result\n     2\t\n     3\tRun folder: `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23/`. It was registered in `SMALL-DESIGN.md` §5 [D750], with council round 66's\n     4\tchanges, and scored by `blind6.py tally` (`TALLY.json`, `reasons.json`). The request is `judging-v13s.txt`, sha256 `54f5847d…`.\n     5\t\n     6\t## 1. Verdict: FAIL on one settled line\n     7\t\n     8\t| Packet | Answer | Shape, rung | First readers (Codex, Claude) | Adjudicators (Codex, Claude) | Settled | Line |\n     9\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n    10\t| X06 | fresh E Claude | forecast, rung 3 ($35B: 1.75x pace) | yes, no | no, no | **no** | **FAIL** |\n    11\t| X07 | fresh E Codex | unresolved | yes, yes | (none) | yes | ok |\n    12\t| X05 | fresh S Codex | forecast, rung 4 ($692.9–737.6B, central about $715B) | yes, yes | (none) | yes | ok |\n    13\t| X08 | fresh S Claude | forecast, rung 4 ($708.1B: $715–745B × 97%) | yes, yes; Codex marked two defects | defects: uphold, reject | yes; two defect splits | inconclusive |\n    14\t| X02 | fresh P-v8 Claude | forecast, rung 2 ($385.9B) | yes, yes | (none) | yes | ok |\n    15\t| X04 | fresh P-v8 Codex | forecast, rung 2 ($377.7B) | yes, yes | (none) | yes | ok |\n    16\t| X03 | control: invented 10% cut | forecast | no, no | (none) | no | ok |\n    17\t| X01 | control: impossible floor | forecast | floor marked by both | (support only) | floor settled | ok |\n    18\t\n    19\t- **The failure.** X06's writer took the market pace (about 1.48–1.5x) as a floor and management's \"more than double\" as a\n    20\t  ceiling, and chose 1.75x, the midpoint.\n    21\t  - Both adjudicators: the passages state \"more than double\" as a minimum; the midpoint has no source; the reasons for a miss\n    22\t    (one customer evaluating, a competitor's own benchmarks, missing volume figures) size or bound no shortfall below $40B. The\n    23\t    answer shows only that the outcome might fall short.\n    24\t- **The defect splits on X08.**\n    25\t  - F15 = 130 ÷ (125–145) gives a range whose top end is 104%. The Codex adjudicator calls that a part above its whole. The Claude\n    26\t    adjudicator calls it the mechanical end of a range ratio that the format requires.\n    27\t  - F5, the outside total-capex range, is marked as the market's reference for data-center spending. The Codex adjudicator says this\n    28\t    implies a 100% share. The Claude adjudicator says F5 is a quoted outside figure, as the reference rule allows, and that the\n    29\t    deduction is set out separately.\n    30\t- **The reasons and premises** behind every settled S and E vote were read and recorded (`reasons.json`). X05 and X08's readers read\n    31\t  the same four companies' AI-infrastructure plan as a plan that can slip, not as a floor, which fits each answer's own outcome.\n    32\t- **A run incident, disclosed.** The background command running the eight Codex readers was terminated (exit 144) while three\n    33\t  readers were still running. X04, X05 and X08 had empty outputs, kept in `reader-packets/interrupted/`. Fresh Codex readers were\n    34\t  run for those three. No reading was discarded after it was seen.\n    35\t\n    36\t## 2. The E record across the four runs (same case, same passages)\n    37\t\n    38\t| Run | Codex writer | Claude writer |\n    39\t|---|---|---|\n    40\t| third run (D742 request) | 85% realisation, $34B; settled no | 1.85x bracket, $37B; yes in re-read 2, split under D747 |\n    41\t| targeted test (D745) | 90% realisation, $36B; settled no | 1.8x × 90% share, $32.4B; split, then no under D747 |\n    42\t| D749 | unresolved, specific gap; settled yes | 2.1x in full, $42B, asserting \"already selling at volume\"; split |\n    43\t| D750 | unresolved, specific gap; settled yes | 1.75x bracket midpoint, $35B; settled no |\n    44\t\n    45\t- **The Codex writer.** The repair (D749, D750) moved it from invented cuts to a justified unresolved, twice.\n    46\t- **The Claude writer.** It forecasts every time, by a different route each time, and has never been settled supported under the\n    47\t  current definitions.\n    48\t- **S** improved but did not fully settle (corrected after council 67):\n    49\t  - D750's two fresh forecasts earned settled support, with two defect marks on X08 still split.\n    50\t  - D749's X08 support verdict was split after the correction: one reader accepted $710.4B while treating $730B as a floor.\n    51\t  - D749's S Codex was settled supported.\n    52\t- **P-v8** has been supported throughout.\n    53\t\n    54\t## 3. Not done\n    55\t\n    56\tNothing was paid. Nothing was tuned on the 36 stored answers. The request was not changed after the registration.\n     1\t# The D749 writer test of the E writer repair: result\n     2\t\n     3\tRun folder: `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/`. It was registered in `SMALL-DESIGN.md` §5 [D749], with council round 64's\n     4\tchanges, and scored by `blind5.py tally` (`TALLY.json`, `reasons.json`). The request is `judging-v13s.txt`, sha256 `ac707fa9…`.\n     5\t\n     6\t## 1. Verdict: INCONCLUSIVE (two support splits, X05 and X08); no line failed\n     7\t\n     8\t**A correction, made after council round 65 (`codex-r65.md`).** I first recorded X08's two \"yes\" readings as compatible, although\n     9\tmy own note named the tension. The Claude reader read e1947 as a floor (\"at least $730B\") while accepting $710.4B. The Codex reader\n    10\tread it as a guide. Under the registered procedure, matching votes with incompatible reasons go to adjudication.\n    11\t- X08 was sent to adjudication. Both adjudicators said yes. The Claude adjudicator again read a floor above the outcome, and the\n    12\t  Codex adjudicator did not reconcile the $730B figure.\n    13\t- X08 is recorded as split (`reasons.json`). The row below is corrected, and the verdict stays INCONCLUSIVE.\n    14\t\n    15\t| Packet | Answer | Shape, rung | First readers (Codex, Claude) | Adjudicators (Codex, Claude) | Settled | Line |\n    16\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n    17\t| X06 | fresh E Codex | unresolved | yes, yes | (none) | yes | ok |\n    18\t| X05 | fresh E Claude | forecast, rung 2 ($42B: 2.1x, 100% Vera) | no, yes | no, yes | split | inconclusive |\n    19\t| X02 | fresh S Codex | forecast, rung 4 ($730B estimate from the same four companies' AI-infrastructure plan) | yes, yes | (none) | yes | ok |\n    20\t| X08 | fresh S Claude | forecast, rung 4 ($740B × 96% = $710.4B) | yes, yes (reasons incompatible: floor against guide) | yes, yes (reasons incompatible again) | split | inconclusive |\n    21\t| X01 | fresh P-v8 Claude | forecast, rung 2 ($383.9B) | yes, yes | (none) | yes | ok |\n    22\t| X04 | fresh P-v8 Codex | forecast, rung 2 ($380.8B) | yes, yes | (none) | yes | ok |\n    23\t| X03 | control: invented 10% cut (blind4 U05) | forecast | no, no | (none) | no | ok |\n    24\t| X07 | control: impossible floor (blind4 U02) | forecast | floor marked by Codex only | floor upheld by both | floor settled | ok |\n    25\t\n    26\t- **The code** accepted all six fresh replies, and all six are publishable.\n    27\t- **No defect** was marked on a fresh reply.\n    28\t- **The reasons** behind the five settled fresh pairs and the two controls were read by Claude and recorded as compatible\n    29\t  (`reasons.json`). X08 is corrected to incompatible, as above.\n    30\t\n    31\t## 2. What changed against the targeted test (D745), same cases\n    32\t\n    33\t- **E.** The Codex writer no longer invents a cut. It answers unresolved and names the gap: both the FY2027 base and the FY2028\n    34\t  increase are projections, and no passage gives a shipment run-rate, an order with units and prices, or a deployment schedule. Both\n    35\t  readers accept it. In the targeted test, the same writer type cut the reference by 10% and was settled not supported.\n    36\t- **S.** Both fresh forecasts are supported by both families. In the targeted test both split.\n    37\t- **P-v8.** Both fresh replies are supported forecasts, as before. The repair did not cause withholding on the supported case.\n    38\t- **The controls.** Both read as expected.\n    39\t\n    40\t## 3. The one split, read against its passages (Claude's own trace reading)\n    41\t\n    42\t- **The answer.** E's Claude writer takes management's \"more than double\" in full, at 2.1x on $20B with Vera as 100%, giving $42B,\n    43\t  rung 2. It argues the FY2027 base \"is reaffirmed for the current year, so the CPU line is already selling at volume rather than a\n    44\t  product with no shipments\".\n    45\t- **The passages it saw (15).** None shows Vera shipments, reported Vera revenue or orders:\n    46\t  - e4689: \"will be deployed\";\n    47\t  - e5044: Anthropic \"is evaluating\";\n    48\t  - e4856: the $20B is a \"potential\";\n    49\t  - e3660: management \"still expects\" $20B, which is an outlook;\n    50\t  - the AMD benchmarks (e5399–e5404) show only that a Vera platform exists to be tested.\nzsh:4: no matches found: docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/REREAD*.md\n     1\tYou are a senior hedge-fund analyst judging the current rungs of a family of related number theses: one company's quantities that overlap, such as a total and its segment, a level and its growth, or a later year that rests on an earlier year's base. Use only the supplied evidence and themes in force. Each rung is your forecast: the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved.\n     2\t\n     3\t- Judge every member from the evidence shown (what recent judgements relied on and what arrived recently) and the themes in force, as if for the first time. Earlier forecasts are not shown: whether a conclusion changed is compared afterwards.\n     4\t- Judge each member against a reference: what the market expects for that question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on its ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate whose pace you forecast, informed by guidance, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted subject to the evidence requirement below; it remains a forecast when reaffirmed, and is taken in full only when you show what evidence puts the required volume in hand for the period. Build the family's figures once and read every member's outcome from them: a part cannot exceed its whole, a figure that spans several periods or products gives a question only its share, and a quantity the family shares has one forecast. Compare each outcome with its rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\n     5\t- Weigh by tier and by novelty. Only fact can establish that a rung's outcome already holds; \"not established\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. Guidance for the current or next quarter is the strongest forecast input. A management target for a product not yet shipping, or an ambition spanning years, is an input, not an answer: weigh time and execution (how many selling quarters remain, what volume and price the figure implies, what the company's own choices say) under the evidence requirement below; consensus is where the market stands, not the answer either. third_party_view and rumour move the forecast in proportion to their specificity (a number, a date, a named party): a research firm's shipment or revenue tracking with a number counts. A commitment or deal that is announced but not sized is sized from its disclosed comparable (the same programme's other deal, scaled by capacity or units); a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it. A repeat, another outlet for the same claim, or an opinion that adds no new fact or calculation moves nothing. Missing confirmation alone is not grounds for the lowest or highest rung: an event not being confirmed is not confirmation that it did not happen.\n     6\t- An evidence item that replaces another includes its predecessor for context, such as guidance revised from 12% to 25% growth. The predecessor is no longer evidence in force.\n     7\t- \"Themes in force\" lists the library's open theme theses (policy stages, shared quantities) with their current conclusions. They are our own judgements, never evidence: do not cite them in cites. Weigh each one that bears on a member and report it in that member's theme_pushes with the direction it pushes that member (toward_first, toward_last or none); leave out a theme that does not bear on it. A theme marked \"updated since this thesis was last judged\" may change the conclusion; one that is not marked already stood at the previous judgement. When no themes are listed, theme_pushes is empty.\n     8\t- Your conclusions must be consistent with each other and with the themes in force as shown: a share cannot be judged at zero while the policy that gates it is judged likely to open.\n     9\t- Return the family's figures and one answer per member, as the JSON schema defines them. Every number an answer rests on is a figure; a number you judge rather than read or compute is an estimate, stated as yours. Before sizing a share, deduction, discount or pace that decides the band, explain which band the evidence favours. Show support for the size or a justifying bound. If the passages lack the quantity asked, explain why a related figure fits the companies, period and quantity, and why your assumptions favour that band. If you can say only that the outcome might fall short or overshoot, not which band is more likely, answer unresolved and name what is unknown. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\n10:- e122 [third_party_view; Autonocion.com; original source Tom’s Hardware ; from gnews:417f6244f0aaa27123023deb 2026-07-06] Tom’s Hardware’s running tally said more than 75 U.S. data-center projects worth around $130 billion had been blocked or delayed so far in 2026.\n11:    Quote: More than 75 of these projects, worth around $130 billion, have been blocked or delayed across the US so far this year, according to a running tally at Tom’s Hardware .\n29:- e1947 [party_statement; fool.com (Patrick Sanders); original source Amazon, Meta Platforms, Microsoft, and Alphabet ; from gnews:c8ac167e2356a75994ebc26f 2026-08-13] Amazon, Meta Platforms, Microsoft, and Alphabet indicated they will spend more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure in calendar 2026.\n30:    Quote: hyperscalers Amazon , Meta Platforms , Microsoft , and Alphabet have indicated they will spend more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure this year\n32:- e2079 [party_statement; uk.finance.yahoo.com; original source Microsoft, Amazon, Alphabet, Meta and other hyperscalers ; from gnews:1219126db2e6631aa1264142 2026-08-16] Microsoft, Amazon, Alphabet, Meta and other hyperscalers guided combined 2026 capital spending of roughly $720 billion to $745 billion, about 77% above the prior year.\n40:    Quote: With cloud industry backlog now greater than $2 trillion, CapEx by the top 5 hyperscalers is expected to reach nearly $800 billion in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n52:- e4405 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source Microsoft ; from gnews:9c64c0df4ef80362d10066d5 2026-09-06] Microsoft reported a $678 billion backlog for its Azure cloud platform.\n53:    Quote: Microsoft is sitting on a $678 billion backlog for its Azure cloud platform\n83:- e5014 [third_party_view; Zacks.com; original source Zacks.com 2026-09-14; from gnews:1d3bc1ba4c2df5e998884bdb 2026-09-14] Zacks estimated that Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft and Meta would spend roughly $720 billion to $745 billion on capital expenditures in calendar 2026, with a large portion directed to AI infrastructure.\n84:    Quote: Four top hyperscalers — Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft and Meta Platforms — alone are expected to spend roughly $720 billion to $745 billion on capital expenditures in 2026, with a large portion directed toward AI infrastructure.\n89:**Start-state correction (2026-11-05):** Alex requires a research library established from real material available before the test start, then extraction against that state. The prior empty-library diagnostic cannot represent this case. Our own mistakes and the current 68-question provenance audit are in `../hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review/docs/research/2026-11-05-parallel-refinement/own-mistakes-2026-11-05.zh.md`. Two new A-only calls were run on 2026-08-29 articles using the 68-question prior-day snapshot; both proposed the same aggregate supply-commitments topic. A source/context review judged it distinct from the existing specific SK Hynix question. A third A-only call on an author-body excerpt about DLSS 5, already tracked by v22, passed; the prompt also restricts software updates, so the reason is not established. No writer or dedup ran; source-history completeness remains unverified, and this is not scored acceptance. Details: ../hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review/docs/research/2026-11-05-parallel-refinement/start-state-diagnostic-2026-11-05.zh.md.\n111:The small 2026-11-05 study used three archived NVDA articles and an 8/28 historical 68-question snapshot: without versus with an appended report, independent AI review found 14/27 versus 13/27 complete matters. This measured appended extraction context, not the distinct former coverage-start step; D370 removes both by Alex's decision. Full traces and limitations are in the separate `hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review` worktree under `docs/research/2026-11-05-real-input-evidence/existing-library-results-2026-11-05.md`.\n135:- **Served data:** the service, the MCP server and the standalone page serve the sealed three-month ledger `served-2026-10-28-v34-nvda-3m-through-0828` (68 theses, 34 groups; D358), built by the earlier `poc-v34` pipeline, not by the replay default. HTTP `/library`, MCP `thesis_library` and the standalone page share the dated bundle writer.\n145:4. **A thesis opened from an article gets no dependency of its own**: the default Jev matching asks for none (`views/jev_matching.py`), although D51 has the matching call choose them; themes still reach every company thesis through the theme block (D191, D195). Found in the 2026-11-05 consolidation; building it is decided (D368).\n206:- **D368 item 2, the matching step chooses a new question's dependencies.** In the unseeded fresh week one clear case among 97\n217:- **D368 item 4, the reading direction for preset questions** (settled by Claude on Alex's delegation): not measured (D536); the\n327:- **Gate 1's reading repeated (D568, D569):** the same 39 sends read again from identical packets give the same line verdicts; line 2's\n433:**The merge test (D678–D680, 2026-11-19): closed; the registered test did not pass.** The old system opens questions, clean Step 1 reads\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/DECISIONS-log.md | sed -n '11592,11619p'; nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/claude-r67.md | head -25; for f in poc/data/judging-v13s-blind[56]-*/S/answers/codex.json; do echo \\\"\"'$f\"; rg -n '\"'\\\"basis_text\\\": \\\"' \\\"\"'$f\"; done'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/DECISIONS-log.md | sed -n '11592,11619p'; nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/claude-r67.md | head -25; for f in poc/data/judging-v13s-blind[56]-*/S/answers/codex.json; do echo \\\"\"'$f\"; rg -n '\"'\\\"basis_text\\\": \\\"' \\\"\"'$f\"; done'","aggregated_output":" 11592\t## D748 · 2026-11-23 · The D747 re-read fails on a self-contradicting floor; three of four earlier splits settle under the ruling; council 63: ask Alex to authorize the E writer repair; the paid re-send stays on hold\n 11593\t\n 11594\t- **The result** (`docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND4-RESULT.md` §6; `REREAD-TALLY.json`, `reread-reasons.json`):\n 11595\t  - **FAIL on the stop line.** On U02 (S, Claude writer), both adjudicators upheld \"part above or added into its whole\". The answer\n 11596\t    calls \"more than $730B\" of AI infrastructure \"the supported floor\" for data-center spending, while its own total spending is $720B\n 11597\t    and its data-center outcome $705.6B.\n 11598\t    - The code did not catch it. The figure is linked only to the 98% share, and the floor is claimed in prose. D738 leaves that\n 11599\t      reading to readers.\n 11600\t    - In the 18 stored blind replies this explicit defect occurs once (Codex, council 63).\n 11601\t  - **Six of nine settle with compatible reasons**, and each fixed reading that settled matched its registration:\n 11602\t    - U01, U03 and U05: no;\n 11603\t    - U06: no;\n 11604\t    - U04 (justified unresolved): yes;\n 11605\t    - U08 ($25B deduction explained by the same companies' AI-infrastructure figure and Meta's share): yes.\n 11606\t  - **Three of the four answers that split in the targeted test now settle** (U04, U06, U08).\n 11607\t  - **Three splits remain:** T10, T15 and U02's support vote.\n 11608\t    - All six adjudicator votes followed their own family's first vote. Which family was stricter changed with the question: Codex on\n 11609\t      S, Claude on E.\n 11610\t    - T15 had been accepted in all three earlier readings.\n 11611\t- **A process error** (Claude and council 62). The registration required five boundary answers to settle, or to settle as expected,\n 11612\t  without estimating how often readers split on boundary answers (AGENTS §7.1, \"a conjunction over many items fails by chance\"). The\n 11613\t  stop on U02 does not depend on it.\n 11614\t- **Council round 63** (`claude-r63.md`, `codex-r63.md`) reached consensus:\n 11615\t  - **The FAIL stands. No further reader round.** Another vote on the same nine answers leaves the writers untouched.\n 11616\t  - **Claude conceded three points.**\n 11617\t    - The remaining splits are not shown to be random variation. T10 and T15 turn on judgement over \"explains why\" and \"more likely\".\n 11618\t      On U02, one side accepted the band and the other required the writer's stated route. D747 already rejects U02's route. No\n 11619\t      missing general clause was found.\n     1\t# Council round 67 (reflection after D750): Claude's reflection, written before Codex's read\n     2\t\n     3\t## Where the line stands (log read: D742–D750, `BLIND3/4/5/6-RESULT.md`)\n     4\t- **P-v8 (data-center revenue).** Supported in every run, by every writer and both reader families.\n     5\t- **S (four companies' data-center capex).** Converged over D749 and D750: all four fresh forecasts are supported. The only open marks\n     6\t  are two defect splits on one answer: a range-ratio end above 100%, and a total-capex reference.\n     7\t- **Controls.** Every invented-cut and impossible-floor control was caught in D749 and D750.\n     8\t- **E (Vera FY2028).** The one case that does not converge (`BLIND6-RESULT.md` §2).\n     9\t  - The Codex writer moved from invented cuts to a justified unresolved, accepted twice.\n    10\t  - The Claude writer forecasts every time, by a new route each time: a bracket, the target in full, a bracket again. It has never been\n    11\t    settled supported. D750 fails on its bracket midpoint.\n    12\t\n    13\t## The model's own account (D750's E Claude writer, asked after the fact)\n    14\t- **Pushed to forecast below guidance by:**\n    15\t  - bullet 2: \"a target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted…\";\n    16\t  - bullet 3: \"weigh time and execution…\";\n    17\t  - \"'not established' … never a reason to stay\".\n    18\t- **Rejected the unresolved sentence** because it believed the market paths gave a bound. It now agrees they do not.\n    19\t- **Would now answer rung 2,** citing \"A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung\", and\n    20\t  notes: \"The passages never say Vera is not shipping.\"\n    21\t- **So the discount clause's trigger is undecidable from E's passages.** The writer swings between taking the target in full (rung\n    22\t  2, a split in D749) and cutting it (rung 3, rejected). Only \"unresolved\" has been accepted by both families.\n    23\t\n    24\t## What I take from it\n    25\t1. **This is the loop AGENTS §1 names.** \"When one mechanism draws findings round after round, stop answering findings: propose one\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/S/answers/codex.json\n93:      \"basis_text\": \"I favor the $700 billion to below $800 billion band. The four companies' AI infrastructure plan covers the right buyers and year. Amazon's memory purchases and the capacity needed to serve Azure's backlog point to servers and the facilities that house them as the main use of that money. For data-center capex to fall below $700 billion, spending outside data centers and spending deferred beyond year-end would together have to absorb more than $30 billion of the indicated plan, even before allowing for conventional cloud equipment bought for the same facilities. The project-delay tally does not identify that much spending due from these buyers this year. I use $730 billion as a rounded estimate near the stated floor of the AI plan. The four-company total capex estimate limits the likely upside, and the broader data-center estimates support the scale without supplying a precise split.\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23/S/answers/codex.json\n29:      \"basis_text\": \"I favor rung 4. Against the four companies’ own AI infrastructure spending indication, falling below $700 billion would require more than $30 billion of that plan to fall outside CY2026 data-center capex, even before counting conventional cloud investment. Amazon tied its higher budget chiefly to memory costs, and the same four companies have signed data-center leases. Both point to spending on the equipment and facilities the question covers. I estimate that 93–99% of tracked total capex reaches data centers. The upper end allows for conventional cloud facilities as well as AI. The lower end allows for other corporate investment, differences in what the companies call AI infrastructure, and construction delays. The passages do not disclose the actual split.\",\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
