{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -Bc 'from pathlib import Path\nimport os,hashlib,tiktoken\nkey=hashlib.sha1(b\\\"https://openaipublic.blob.core.windows.net/encodings/o200k_base.tiktoken\\\").hexdigest()\nfor v in [\\\"TIKTOKEN_CACHE_DIR\\\",\\\"DATA_GYM_CACHE_DIR\\\",\\\"TMPDIR\\\"]:\n if not os.getenv(v):continue\n path=Path(os.getenv(v));path=path/\\\"data-gym-cache\\\" if v==\\\"TMPDIR\\\" else path\n if (path/key).is_file():\n  os.environ[\\\"TIKTOKEN_CACHE_DIR\\\"]=str(path)\n  enc=tiktoken.get_encoding(\\\"o200k_base\\\");a=Path(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/judging-v13s.txt\\\").read_text();b=a.replace(\\\"Before sizing a share, deduction, discount or pace that decides the band, explain which band the evidence favours.\\\",\\\"When other evidence bears on the forecast, weigh the plausible alternatives for any unknown fact that decides the band. Explain whether the evidence as a whole favours one band before deciding whether or how much to adjust.\\\");print(len(enc.encode(a)),len(enc.encode(b)));break'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -Bc 'from pathlib import Path\nimport os,hashlib,tiktoken\nkey=hashlib.sha1(b\\\"https://openaipublic.blob.core.windows.net/encodings/o200k_base.tiktoken\\\").hexdigest()\nfor v in [\\\"TIKTOKEN_CACHE_DIR\\\",\\\"DATA_GYM_CACHE_DIR\\\",\\\"TMPDIR\\\"]:\n if not os.getenv(v):continue\n path=Path(os.getenv(v));path=path/\\\"data-gym-cache\\\" if v==\\\"TMPDIR\\\" else path\n if (path/key).is_file():\n  os.environ[\\\"TIKTOKEN_CACHE_DIR\\\"]=str(path)\n  enc=tiktoken.get_encoding(\\\"o200k_base\\\");a=Path(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/judging-v13s.txt\\\").read_text();b=a.replace(\\\"Before sizing a share, deduction, discount or pace that decides the band, explain which band the evidence favours.\\\",\\\"When other evidence bears on the forecast, weigh the plausible alternatives for any unknown fact that decides the band. Explain whether the evidence as a whole favours one band before deciding whether or how much to adjust.\\\");print(len(enc.encode(a)),len(enc.encode(b)));break'\"","aggregated_output":"975 993\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"agent_message","text":"**Change the wording first, then run D750’s $0 writer test once.** I agree with Claude’s classification of all eight E replies. The $42B and $35B forecasts use the same gap in opposite directions. The proposed check exposes both, but “across the plausible readings” can also sound as though a forecast must survive *every* plausible reading. Alex explicitly allowed publication when one side is more likely and the other remains possible. [Paper check:22](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAPER-CHECK-D751.md:22) · [D747:11575](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11575)\n\n1. **Serious — the eight E replies support the paper check.** All four runs supplied the same 15 passages. Management reaffirmed an expectation, described future deployments and identified a customer evaluating Vera. The benchmark tests establish that a working platform existed. Those facts leave open both how much was already selling and how much customers would buy in FY2028. [E passages:9](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23/E/user.txt:9) · [Evaluation and benchmarks:33](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23/E/user.txt:33)\n\n   | Stored reply | Independent reading |\n   |---|---|\n   | Third run, Codex, **$34B** | **Exposed.** It takes 85% of the projected $20B base, then doubles $17B. Expected adoption explains “most” but gives no reason to choose a shortfall large enough to put FY2028 below $40B. [Reply:178](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/answers/codex.json:178) |\n   | Third run, Claude, **$37B** | **Boundary case.** It explains why broad adoption favours growth above the market’s roughly 50%, why competition tempers the doubling forecast, and what would take sales above $40B. Readers can reasonably differ on whether that evidence makes a miss *more likely*. Earlier readings accepted it, and the D747 reading split. [Reply:162](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/answers/claude.json:162) · [Reading history:11607](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11607) |\n   | Targeted run, Codex, **$36B** | **Exposed.** Describing the 10% cut as less than half an average quarter translates it into time. It supplies no evidence that this much business will slip or go to a rival. [Reply:130](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json:130) |\n   | Targeted run, Claude, **$32.4B** | **Exposed.** Its sensitivity check varies Vera’s share while holding the 1.8× growth estimate fixed. Its own lower growth comparison gives $20B × 1.5 × 90% = $27B, in a different band. The passages also leave the supposed prior-generation share unsized. [Reply:162](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json:162) |\n   | D749, Codex, **can’t tell** | **Preserved.** Both the base and the increase are projections. The writer explains how a modest shortfall could change the band and identifies the missing shipment pace, priced orders or delivery schedule that could distinguish the outcomes. [Reply:68](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json:68) |\n   | D749, Claude, **$42B** | **Exposed.** “Already selling at volume” is inferred from reaffirmed guidance. The writer uses that premise to take the target in full. Showing that 2.0× to just under 2.5× lands in its chosen band leaves unanswered why sales are likely to reach 2.0×. The original support vote remains split. [Reply:1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json:1) · [Result:18](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND5-RESULT.md:18) |\n   | D750, Codex, **can’t tell** | **Preserved.** The writer explains why customer interest and rising budgets support demand while leaving purchases by the deadline open. It identifies missing shipments against the base, scheduled deliveries, units and realised prices. [Reply:83](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json:83) |\n   | D750, Claude, **$35B** | **Exposed.** It treats market growth as NVIDIA’s floor and “more than double” as its ceiling, then takes the midpoint. Neither endpoint bounds realised sales. Even its 1.48× market comparison gives $29.6B, below its chosen $30–40B band. Both adjudicators rejected the forecast. [Reply:1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json:1) · [Result:19](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND6-RESULT.md:19) |\n\n2. **Minor — the three S forecasts stand, with one correction to Claude’s summary.** The two Codex replies compare the same four buyers’ AI-infrastructure plan with the $700B cutoff. They explain why more than $30B would have to fall outside the relevant spending or year, and why memory purchases, capacity demand and the scope of the reported delays favour clearing the cutoff. D750 Codex expressly allows a lower outcome in its $692.9–737.6B range. D750 Claude makes a different argument for $708.1B. It compares the deduction needed at the midpoint and top of total spending with Meta’s AI spending, Amazon’s memory purchases and raised budgets. The paper’s “$30–45B outside data centers” is too uniform a description of the three answers. All three earned settled **forecast support**. D750 Claude’s total-capex reference and its 104% ratio endpoint still have separate split defect votes. [D749 Codex:93](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/S/answers/codex.json:93) · [D750 Codex:29](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23/S/answers/codex.json:29) · [D750 Claude:1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23/S/answers/claude.json:1) · [Defect votes:24](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND6-RESULT.md:24)\n\n3. **Wording — change one sentence in line 9.** The exact sentence to replace is **“Before sizing a share, deduction, discount or pace that decides the band, explain which band the evidence favours.”** It is the third sentence on the line. Keep the existing routes for a supported size, a justifying bound and an explained comparison when the passages lack the quantity asked. Keep the existing instruction to answer unresolved and name the unknown. That avoids saying it twice. [Current line 9](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/judging-v13s.txt:9) · [D745:11513](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11513) · [D747:11582](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11582)\n\n   - **Guidance alone.** “When other evidence bears on the forecast” preserves the guidance-alone rule. A repeat adds no new fact. When relevant evidence does arrive, the writer weighs it together with guidance and can still reach the same band. [System text:4](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/judging-v13s.txt:4)\n   - **Not yet shipping.** Bullet 2 makes a discount conditional on both shipment status and evidential support. Bullet 3 says to weigh time and execution. On E, a customer evaluation cannot establish that commercial shipments have started or that they have yet to start. The final check should catch an assumption in either direction before the writer keeps or cuts the target. [System text:4](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/judging-v13s.txt:4) · [Writer’s account:13](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/claude-r67.md:13)\n   - **“Not established” and missing confirmation.** The first concerns confidence that an outcome *already holds*. The second prevents missing confirmation from establishing the opposite event or an extreme band. Neither requires a move. A specific unresolved answer explains why the missing fact changes which band to publish. An explained forecast can acknowledge the same uncertainty and still favour one band. [System text:1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/judging-v13s.txt:1) · [System text:5](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/judging-v13s.txt:5) · [D749:11645](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11645)\n   - **Smallest change.** Use “whether” to let the writer find that neither band is favoured. Ask it to *weigh* plausible alternatives so a range may cross a cutoff. This applies equally to sales, a spending share or a growth rate. The proposed full text grows from 4,867 to 4,976 characters, or 975 to 993 `o200k_base` tokens. The added words cover the previously implicit choice to take a target in full. [Existing request size:523](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:523)\n\n4. **Registration — reuse D750’s test and scoring, with round 67’s exit rule.** Pin the changed request before sending. Use one fresh writer from each family on E, S and P-v8, six replies total. Keep the invented-cut and impossible-floor controls with their original requests. Retain blinded readers, adjudication, and the requirement to record the decisive premise and its passage support. A clean pass requires all six replies to be publishable, settled support on both E and S pairs, supported **forecasts** on both P-v8 replies, clear defect votes and both controls read as registered. E and S may each earn support for a specific unresolved answer, so a pass alone cannot establish how often the writer will publish a forecast. [Cases and controls:463](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:463) · [Pass and fail conditions:480](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:480) · [D750 premise checks:537](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:537)\n\n**Verdict.** Revise line 9 as below, then run the one registered writer test. A clean pass goes to Alex for paid authorization. A settled failure or persistent split ends the wording loop and returns the publication choice to Alex. This review used stored replies only. No file changed and no request was sent. [Round 67 exit rule:13](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r67.md:13)\n\n**Exact final text of line 9**\n\n```text\n- Return the family's figures and one answer per member, as the JSON schema defines them. Every number an answer rests on is a figure; a number you judge rather than read or compute is an estimate, stated as yours. When other evidence bears on the forecast, weigh the plausible alternatives for any unknown fact that decides the band. Explain whether the evidence as a whole favours one band before deciding whether or how much to adjust. Show support for the size or a justifying bound. If the passages lack the quantity asked, explain why a related figure fits the companies, period and quantity, and why your assumptions favour that band. If you can say only that the outcome might fall short or overshoot, not which band is more likely, answer unresolved and name what is unknown. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\n```"}}
